(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["mixed",{"l:mixed/why-does-mixing-topics-in-practice-beat-doing-th":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Inside a block of problems from one module you know the technique before you read the statement, so you practise executing it but never practise \u003cb>choosing\u003c/b> it. Mixing problems from different modules (\u003cb>interleaving\u003c/b>) forces the choice, and spreading them over days (\u003cb>spacing\u003c/b>) forces you to rebuild the idea from memory instead of copying it from the last problem. Both feel slower while you practise, and both win clearly on tests weeks later.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Picture learning to drive only in an empty car park, one manoeuvre at a time: twenty hill starts, then twenty reverse parks. Each one goes well because you know what is coming. On a real road nobody announces the manoeuvre; recognising which one the situation needs is half the skill. A problem list filed under \"Sliding window\" is the car park: the label answers the hardest question for you.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Algorithm problems are the same. \"Longest substring with at most k distinct letters\" and \"count the subarrays whose sum is k\" look alike, yet the first is a window and the second needs prefix sums. If you only ever meet them inside their own modules, you never practise telling them apart, and the first time you must, it is in an interview.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Three schedules\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>blocked      W W W W   P P P P   T T T T     \"which idea?\" is answered by the module title\ninterleaved  W P T P   T W P W   P T W T     every problem starts with \"which idea?\"\nspaced       day 1: W P T      day 4: P W T      day 11: T W P\n             (W = window, P = prefix sums, T = two pointers)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch5>What the evidence says\u003c/h5>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Interleaving builds strategy choice.\u003c/b> Rohrer, Dedrick and Stershic gave 126 seventh-grade students the same maths problems over three months, arranged either in blocks or interleaved. Interleaving won on a surprise test a day later (effect size d = 0.42) and by more thirty days later (d = 0.79). Their explanation is the one above: with blocked practice, students know the strategy before they read the problem.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>It holds in real classrooms.\u003c/b> In a preregistered trial with 54 classes over four months, the interleaved group scored 61% against 38% on an unannounced test a month later (Rohrer and colleagues, 2020).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Mix look-alikes, not everything.\u003c/b> A 2019 meta-analysis (Brunmair and Richter) found the benefit is largest when the categories are easy to confuse. That is why this guide pairs confusable techniques: window and prefix sums, BFS and Dijkstra, greedy and DP.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Spacing beats more of the same.\u003c/b> Spreading ten problems over two sessions virtually doubled scores four weeks later, while doing nine problems instead of three in one sitting made no difference at one or four weeks (Rohrer and Taylor, 2006).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>It feels worse while it works better.\u003c/b> In a study summarised by Dunlosky, students who practised in blocks scored higher during practice, yet those who interleaved did three times better on the test a week later. Expect mixed practice to feel slow; that is the effect, not a sign of failure.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch5>How this page builds it in\u003c/h5>\u003cul>\u003cli>The \u003cb>review sets\u003c/b> in this module have no topic labels. Each opens when you have marked every card of the module it follows as done: set 1 after Prefix sums, set 2 after Monotonic stacks, set 3 after Tries, set 4 after Weighted shortest paths, set 5 after Greedy, set 6 after Bits and number theory. A set only uses ideas you have met.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Today's session\u003c/b> has one review slot. It offers the oldest problem or card you marked \u003cb>Brute only\u003c/b> or \u003cb>Stuck\u003c/b> that has become due (three days after you marked it). When nothing is due and a set is open, it offers the next unsolved problem of the earliest open set. One mixed problem a day spreads the sets out on their own.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The hints open one at a time: what to notice, then a nudge, then the key insight, which starts with the \u003cb>Pattern\u003c/b> line. Name the technique yourself, in writing, before you open anything.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Interleaving costs no extra problems; it changes the order. The real costs are two. Each problem takes longer, because the first two to five minutes go on choosing an idea. And your success rate during practice drops, which feels like going backwards. Budget one mixed problem per session: a Medium has a 30-minute timebox, so five sessions a week is about two and a half hours of mixed practice. Compare that with twenty window problems in one weekend: after the first three or four, the extra problems add almost nothing a month later (the overlearning result above).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>After you have met at least two techniques that can be confused. A brand-new technique is learnt best from a worked example and a few problems in a row (the cards and their Labs), and only then mixed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Keep short blocked bursts where they help: two or three problems right after a card get the mechanics into your hands. Then move on.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Situation\u003c/th>\u003cth>Do this\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>You have just read a card\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Its 2-3 Lab problems, in a row\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>A module is finished\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Its review set opens: one mixed problem per session\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Preparing for interviews\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Mostly mixed problems, timed\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>A technique you keep failing\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One short blocked burst, then back to mixed\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Turning three topic pools into one interleaved week with the template below: always take the pool with the most problems left, unless it was the one just used.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start: three pools; nothing chosen yet\u003c/p>\u003cpre>window: w1 w2 w3    prefix: p1 p2    twoptr: t1 t2\norder:  (empty)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">window has the most left (3), so take {{w1}}\u003c/p>\u003cpre>window: w2 w3    prefix: p1 p2    twoptr: t1 t2\norder:  \u003cmark>w1\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">All pools have 2 left; window was just used anyway and ties go by name, so {{p1}}\u003c/p>\u003cpre>window: w2 w3    prefix: p2    twoptr: t1 t2\norder:  w1 \u003cmark>p1\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">window and twoptr have 2 left; take {{t1}} (window would also do; ties go by name)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>window: w2 w3    prefix: p2    twoptr: t2\norder:  w1 p1 \u003cmark>t1\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">window has the most left (2): {{w2}}\u003c/p>\u003cpre>window: w3    prefix: p2    twoptr: t2\norder:  w1 p1 t1 \u003cmark>w2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">One each left: {{p2}}, {{t2}}, then {{w3}}; no two neighbours share a topic\u003c/p>\u003cpre>order:  w1 p1 t1 w2 \u003cmark>p2\u003c/mark> \u003cmark>t2\u003c/mark> \u003cmark>w3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import heapq\n\n\ndef interleave(pools):\n    \"\"\"pools: topic -&gt; list of problems. Returns [(topic, problem)] with no two neighbours from the\n    same topic whenever that is possible: take the largest remaining pool that is not the last topic.\"\"\"\n    queues = {t: list(v) for t, v in pools.items() if v}\n    heap = [(-len(v), t) for t, v in queues.items()]\n    heapq.heapify(heap)\n    out, last = [], None\n    while heap:\n        left, topic = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        if topic == last and heap:\n            other = heapq.heappop(heap)\n            heapq.heappush(heap, (left, topic))\n            left, topic = other\n        out.append((topic, queues[topic].pop(0)))\n        last = topic\n        if left + 1 &lt; 0:\n            heapq.heappush(heap, (left + 1, topic))\n    return out\n\n\nweek = interleave({\"window\": [\"w1\", \"w2\", \"w3\"], \"prefix\": [\"p1\", \"p2\"], \"twoptr\": [\"t1\", \"t2\"]})\n# [('window', 'w1'), ('prefix', 'p1'), ('twoptr', 't1'), ('window', 'w2'), ('prefix', 'p2'), ...]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Use it to build your own mixed sets from the problem banks of finished modules. It keeps each topic's order, and a clean result (no neighbours sharing a topic) exists exactly when no pool holds more than half of all problems, rounded up.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Peeking at the label.\u003c/b> The problem link opens the judge, which may show topic tags; do not scroll to them before you have chosen. The Solutions and Video links name the technique too.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Grading generously.\u003c/b> Needing the nudge means the choice was not yours. Mark Brute only or Stuck so the problem comes back.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Binge-solving a set.\u003c/b> Five mixed problems in one evening is blocked practice again, just with variety; the spacing is gone.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Struggling too long.\u003c/b> Difficulty helps only while it stays achievable. If after ten minutes you cannot name even two candidate ideas, open the first hint.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Mixing only unrelated topics.\u003c/b> The gain comes from look-alikes. A set of a heap problem, a trie problem and a bit trick trains less choice than a window problem next to a prefix-sum problem.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">During a week of blocked practice your accuracy is 90%; in a week of mixed practice it is 60%. Which week taught you more, and why is the accuracy misleading?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Very likely the mixed week. Blocked accuracy is inflated because the block tells you the technique, so you never had to choose; the studies above show blocked groups doing better during practice and much worse weeks later. Mixed accuracy measures the skill you need, including the choice.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does this guide interleave window problems with prefix-sum problems rather than with, say, trie problems?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Interleaving helps most when the categories are easy to confuse (Brunmair and Richter's meta-analysis). Window and prefix-sum problems share their surface (\"subarray\", \"sum\", \"longest\"), so mixing them forces you to find the deciding feature; a trie problem is rarely mistaken for either.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Set 1 is open. You marked one problem Brute only on Monday evening and nothing else is marked. What does the review slot of Today's session offer on Tuesday, and what on Thursday evening?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>On Tuesday nothing is due yet (an item becomes due three days after you marked it), so the slot offers the next unsolved problem of set 1. By Thursday evening the Monday item is due, so it is offered first, as a re-solve from a blank file.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">You have three free hours this weekend and the DP modules are fresh. Plan A: twelve DP problems on Saturday. Plan B: four problems on Saturday and four on Sunday, mixing DP with earlier modules. Which do you choose?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Plan B. Spreading practice over two sessions roughly doubled retention four weeks later in Rohrer and Taylor's study, while extra problems in one sitting added nothing; mixing in earlier modules also trains the choice between DP and its look-alikes (greedy, backtracking).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Do the first three problems of review set 1, one per session if you can. Before writing any code, write down the idea you think applies and a one-line reason; compare it with the Pattern line only after solving.\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>219 Contains Duplicate II.\u003c/b> Expected: false for \u003ccode>nums = [4, 1, 2, 4], k = 2\u003c/code> (the two 4s are three apart) and true for \u003ccode>[4, 1, 4, 2]\u003c/code>; target O(n) time.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>1679 Max Number of K-Sum Pairs.\u003c/b> Expected: 2 for \u003ccode>nums = [2, 2, 3, 3, 3], k = 5\u003c/code>, and 2 for \u003ccode>[1, 1, 1, 1], k = 2\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>930 Binary Subarrays With Sum.\u003c/b> Expected: 6 for \u003ccode>nums = [1, 0, 0, 1], goal = 1\u003c/code>, and 6 for \u003ccode>[0, 1, 0, 1], goal = 1\u003c/code>; target O(n).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Expected for the whole Lab: you named an idea within about two minutes for each problem, and your written guess matched the Pattern line at least twice.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know why mixed, spaced practice beats blocks, and how this page's review sets and Today's session schedule it for you.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:mixed/which-technique-does-a-problem-statement-point-t":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Read a statement for three things, in this order: the \u003cb>constraints\u003c/b> (which running times are even allowed), the \u003cb>shape of the answer\u003c/b> (one number, a count, every solution, a structure) and the \u003cb>structure of the input\u003c/b> (sorted, contiguous, pairs, grid, graph, stream). Together they usually leave two candidates; a \u003cb>counter-cue\u003c/b> decides between them. The atlas below lists the cues and counter-cues for every technique in this guide.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A doctor does not test for every disease. Symptoms suggest a short list, and one deciding test rules out the look-alike. Statement cues are the symptoms (\"longest contiguous\", \"fewest moves\", \"every way\") and counter-cues are the deciding tests (\"values can be negative\", \"moves cost different amounts\").\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>The two-minute routine\u003c/h5>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Constraints.\u003c/b> Turn n into a target complexity (the template below). n &lt;= 20 invites exponential search; n = 10^5 demands O(n log n); n = 10^9 means you cannot even read n items.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Answer shape.\u003c/b> Every solution listed points to search; a count or a best value over choices points to DP or greedy; a yes/no on a number that only flips once points to binary search on the answer.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Input structure.\u003c/b> Sorted? Contiguous? Intervals? A grid or links? A stream?\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Atlas row, then its counter-cue.\u003c/b> If the counter-cue fires, move to the row it names.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Nothing fits?\u003c/b> Write the brute force and climb from it (the module Deriving solutions to unfamiliar problems).\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch5>The atlas\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Statement cue\u003c/th>\u003cth>Technique\u003c/th>\u003cth>Module\u003c/th>\u003cth>Counter-cue, then use\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>No idea at all, n tiny\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Brute force with itertools\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Solving problems you have not seen\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Always a valid first step and your test oracle\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\"Seen this value or its complement before?\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Set or dict lookup\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hash tables, sets and counting\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Need order, ranges or nearest values: sort or an ordered map\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Count, group, anagram, duplicates\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Counter or a canonical key\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hash tables, sets and counting\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Only the top k counts: add a heap\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Values in 1..n, O(1) extra space\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Array as its own index\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Arrays, dynamic arrays, strings and grids\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Values unbounded: hashing\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Rotate, transpose, spiral, simulate on a matrix\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Index arithmetic and reversals\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Arrays, dynamic arrays, strings and grids\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fewest moves across the grid: BFS\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Brackets, nesting, undo, most recent first\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Stack\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Linked lists, stacks and queues\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"Next greater\": monotonic stack\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Next pointers; cycle, middle, k-th from the end\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Pointer rewiring, fast and slow pointers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Linked lists, stacks and queues\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Random access needed: copy to a list\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sorted input, pair or triple with a target\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Two pointers from both ends\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Two pointers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Unsorted and indices wanted: hash map\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Remove, compact or partition in place\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Read and write pointers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Two pointers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Extra space allowed and order kept: build a new list\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Longest or shortest contiguous stretch under a condition\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Variable sliding window\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Sliding window\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Negative values or an exact target: prefix sums\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Every window of length k\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fixed window\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Sliding window\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Window maximum: monotonic deque\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Count subarrays with sum, remainder or balance exactly k\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Prefix sums plus a count map\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Prefix sums and difference arrays\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Non-negative values and \"at most\": a window works too\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Many range sums on unchanging data\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1D or 2D prefix sums\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Prefix sums and difference arrays\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Updates between queries: Fenwick tree\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Many range additions, one read at the end\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Difference array\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Prefix sums and difference arrays\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Reads between updates: Fenwick tree\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sorted or rotated array, find a position\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Binary search on the index\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Binary search and searching the answer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Unsorted, one query: just scan\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Minimise the maximum, smallest x that works\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Binary search on the answer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Binary search and searching the answer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Check not monotone in x: DP or search\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Next greater or smaller, \"until a taller one\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Monotonic stack\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Monotonic stacks and queues\u003c/td>\u003ctd>k-th greater rather than next: ordered structure\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Maximum or minimum of every window\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Monotonic deque\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Monotonic stacks and queues\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A single window: one scan\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sum of the minimum over all subarrays\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Contribution counting with a monotonic stack\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Monotonic stacks and queues\u003c/td>\u003ctd>n &lt;= 1000: running minimum over all subarrays\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Order would make it easy: neighbours, groups\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Sort first, then one pass\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Sorting, partitioning and selection\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Data arrives over time: heap or ordered map\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>k-th smallest of fixed data, asked once\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Quickselect, heapq.nsmallest or sort\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Sorting, partitioning and selection\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Many queries or a stream: heap\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Repeatedly take the best while items arrive\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Heap\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Heaps and priority queues\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One query on fixed data: sort or select\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Merge k sorted sources; running median\u003c/td>\u003ctd>k-way merge; two heaps\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Heaps and priority queues\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Arbitrary deletions: ordered map\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Start and end pairs: merge, overlap, free time\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Sort by start and merge\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Intervals and sweep line\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Peak overlap: sweep events\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Rooms needed, peak load\u003c/td>\u003ctd>+1/-1 event sweep or a heap of end times\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Intervals and sweep line\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Weighted intervals: DP\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Most non-overlapping intervals\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Earliest end first\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Intervals and sweep line\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Intervals carry values: DP with bisect\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Root and children; answer combines subtrees\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Recursion returning values up\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Binary trees and tree recursion\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Answer depends on the path from the root: pass state down\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Level by level, nearest to the root\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Breadth-first traversal by levels\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Binary trees and tree recursion\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Path-shaped questions: DFS\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sorted data with inserts and deletes; floor, ceiling, rank\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Ordered map: SortedList or bisect\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Binary search trees and ordered maps\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No updates: sorted list plus bisect\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Many words sharing prefixes; words in a grid\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Trie\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tries\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Exact lookups only: a set\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Grid cells or linked entities; reachability, components\u003c/td>\u003ctd>BFS or DFS flood fill\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Graph modelling and traversal\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Links only added, queries online: union-find\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Fewest moves, every move costs the same\u003c/td>\u003ctd>BFS, multi-source if many starts\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Graph modelling and traversal\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Moves cost different amounts: Dijkstra\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Prerequisites, dependency order\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Topological sort\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Graph modelling and traversal\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Undirected: union-find or parent tracking\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Two groups with \"must differ\" pairs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Two-colouring\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Graph modelling and traversal\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"Must be together\" pairs: union-find\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\"Same group?\" while links are added\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Union-find\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Union-find and minimum spanning trees\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Need paths or distances: BFS\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Connect everything at least total cost\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Kruskal or Prim\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Union-find and minimum spanning trees\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One cheapest route: shortest paths\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Weighted edges, cheapest route, weights &gt;= 0\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Dijkstra\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Weighted shortest paths\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Negative edges: Bellman-Ford; costs 0 or 1: 0-1 BFS\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>At most k stops\u003c/td>\u003ctd>k rounds of Bellman-Ford, or Dijkstra on (node, stops)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Weighted shortest paths\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No hop limit: Dijkstra\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>List all, every, generate; n around 20\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Backtracking with pruning\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Complete search and backtracking\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Only a count or best: DP\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Count ways or optimise over choices; states repeat\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Memoised recursion, then a table\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Dynamic programming: from recursion to tables\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One choice provably safe: greedy\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Modes: holding or not, cooldown\u003c/td>\u003ctd>State-machine DP\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Dynamic programming: from recursion to tables\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No modes: take or skip\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Two sequences: subsequence, edit, align\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Two-sequence DP table\u003c/td>\u003ctd>DP on grids, strings and knapsacks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Contiguous only: window or hashing\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Right and down moves on a grid\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Grid DP\u003c/td>\u003ctd>DP on grids, strings and knapsacks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Moves in all directions: graph search\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Items, weights, capacity or target sum\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Knapsack DP\u003c/td>\u003ctd>DP on grids, strings and knapsacks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Huge capacity, n &lt;= 40: meet in the middle\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>A local choice looks safe; reach, fuel, balance\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Greedy with an exchange argument\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Greedy algorithms and exchange arguments\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A small counterexample breaks it: DP\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Burst or merge over a range; subsets of &lt;= 20; digits of N\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Interval, bitmask or digit DP\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Advanced dynamic programming\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Simple choices: ordinary DP\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\"Implement a class\" with costs per operation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hash map plus a list, heap or sorted list\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Designing data structures\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One operation only: one structure\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Find a pattern in a long text; repeats\u003c/td>\u003ctd>KMP or rolling hash\u003c/td>\u003ctd>String algorithms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Short text: str.find\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Once while others appear twice; subsets as masks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>XOR and bitmasks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Bits and number theory\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Other multiplicities: count bits per position\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Modulo 10^9 + 7, gcd, primes, nCr\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Modular arithmetic, sieve, combinations\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Bits and number theory\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Small values: compute directly\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Range queries mixed with updates\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fenwick or segment tree\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Range queries: Fenwick and segment trees\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No updates: prefix sums or a sparse table\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The routine takes about two minutes. A wrong technique typically costs 20 to 30 minutes before you notice, so even one avoided miss per week pays for the habit many times over. The template's estimate is O(1) per call: seven formulas.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>On every unfamiliar statement, before writing code; and after every solve, to add or sharpen a row in your own copy. A row only suggests: the counter-cue and a hand-worked example decide. When two rows match, the problem is often both: sort then sweep, Dijkstra then DP over distances, BFS inside binary search.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Classifying a statement nobody labelled: \"n &lt;= 10^5 positive integers; find the length of the shortest contiguous stretch whose sum is at least T.\"\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Constraints first: n = 10^5, so the budget allows {{O(n log n)}}; all pairs (5 * 10^9) is out\u003c/p>\u003cpre>n = 10^5   -&gt;   target O(n log n) or better\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Answer shape: one length, a best value over many stretches\u003c/p>\u003cpre>shape: \u003cmark>optimise over ranges\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Input structure: one array, the stretch must be {{contiguous}}, values all {{positive}}\u003c/p>\u003cpre>structure: contiguous, positive\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Atlas rows that match: \"shortest contiguous stretch\" (window) and \"sum\" (prefix sums)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>candidates: \u003cmark>variable window\u003c/mark>  |  prefix sums\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Counter-cue check: values are positive, so adding grows the sum and removing shrinks it\u003c/p>\u003cpre>window counter-cue (\"negative values\") does \u003cmark>not\u003c/mark> fire\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Decision: variable window, O(n). Change one word, \"integers may be negative\", and the counter-cue fires\u003c/p>\u003cpre>positive -&gt; \u003cmark>window\u003c/mark>\nmay be negative -&gt; \u003cmark>prefix sums + monotonic deque\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import math\n\nCLASSES = [\n    (\"O(n!)\", lambda n: math.factorial(n) if n &lt;= 20 else math.inf),\n    (\"O(2^n)\", lambda n: 2.0 ** n if n &lt;= 1000 else math.inf),\n    (\"O(n^3)\", lambda n: float(n) ** 3),\n    (\"O(n^2)\", lambda n: float(n) ** 2),\n    (\"O(n sqrt n)\", lambda n: n * math.sqrt(n)),\n    (\"O(n log n)\", lambda n: n * max(1.0, math.log2(n))),\n    (\"O(n)\", lambda n: float(n)),\n]\n\n\ndef target_complexity(n, budget=10**7):\n    \"\"\"Slowest complexity class whose operation count fits the budget (about one second of simple\n    Python operations). Anything faster also fits; anything slower will not.\"\"\"\n    for name, ops in CLASSES:\n        if ops(n) &lt;= budget:\n            return name\n    return \"O(log n) or O(1): you cannot even read n items\"\n\n\n# n = 8: O(n!)   20: O(2^n)   1000: O(n^2)   5000: O(n sqrt n)   10^5: O(n log n)\n# 10^6: O(n)     10^9: O(log n) or O(1)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The budget of 10^7 simple operations per second is a rule of thumb for pure Python; work done inside C built-ins (\u003ccode>sorted\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>sum\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>set\u003c/code> operations) runs 10 to 100 times faster.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Matching surface words.\u003c/b> \"Subarray\" does not always mean a window, and a \"tree\" of dependencies may be a general graph.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Ignoring small constraints.\u003c/b> n &lt;= 20 is an invitation: try every subset or permutation, or use bitmasks.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Stopping at the first row.\u003c/b> Always check the counter-cue; it exists because the look-alike is common.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Forgetting combinations.\u003c/b> Many Mediums are two rows at once: sort then two pointers, Dijkstra then DP.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does \u003ccode>target_complexity(2000)\u003c/code> return, and is an O(n^2 log n) idea safe at n = 2000?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\"O(n^2)\": n^3 = 8·10^9 is far over the budget and n^2 = 4·10^6 fits. n^2 log n is about 4.4·10^7 simple operations, several seconds in pure Python, so it is risky; look for O(n^2) or move the inner work into C built-ins.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A colleague classifies \"fewest knight moves between two squares of a chessboard\" as grid DP. Which cue did they miss?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Grid DP needs moves in a fixed direction (right and down), so the cells form an acyclic order. A knight moves in all directions, so the board is a graph with cycles and every move costs one: BFS.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does \"values may be negative\" rule out the variable window for \"shortest stretch with sum at least T\"?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The window relies on monotone validity: removing an element from the left must never make an invalid window valid. With negatives, dropping a negative element raises the sum, so the window may stop shrinking too early (or never become valid) and miss shorter answers.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each problem write the atlas row you used and its counter-cue before coding, then compare with the Pattern line.\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>438 Find All Anagrams in a String.\u003c/b> Expected: \u003ccode>[0, 1, 3]\u003c/code> for \u003ccode>s = \"cbacab\", p = \"abc\"\u003c/code>; target O(n).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>189 Rotate Array.\u003c/b> Expected: \u003ccode>[4, 5, 1, 2, 3]\u003c/code> for \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5], k = 2\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>[3, 1, 2]\u003c/code> for \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3], k = 4\u003c/code> (k larger than n); O(1) extra space.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>1657 Determine if Two Strings Are Close.\u003c/b> Expected: true for \u003ccode>(\"aab\", \"bba\")\u003c/code>, false for \u003ccode>(\"aabbc\", \"abccc\")\u003c/code> and false for \u003ccode>(\"ab\", \"aa\")\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to classify a statement from its constraints, answer shape and input structure, and check the counter-cue before you commit.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:mixed/how-do-you-tell-look-alike-techniques-apart":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Most wrong-technique mistakes come from six pairs that share their surface: sliding window or prefix sums, BFS or Dijkstra, greedy or DP, heap or sort or quickselect, backtracking or DP, union-find or BFS. Each pair has one \u003cb>deciding question\u003c/b>, such as \"does the condition stay true when I shrink the range?\". When you are still unsure, write the simpler idea and let a random tester compare it with a brute force: a two-minute \u003cb>counterexample hunt\u003c/b> beats twenty minutes of arguing.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Identical twins share a face; you tell them apart by one feature, a scar or a voice. You do not compare everything, you check the one thing that differs. Each pair below has such a feature, and it is always a property of the problem, never a word in the statement.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Six pairs and their deciding questions\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Pair\u003c/th>\u003cth>What they share\u003c/th>\u003cth>Deciding question\u003c/th>\u003cth>Yes\u003c/th>\u003cth>No\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sliding window or prefix sums\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Contiguous subarrays, sums\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Does shrinking a valid range keep it valid (and growing an invalid one keep it invalid)?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Window: O(n), O(1) space\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Prefix sums plus a hash map: exact targets, negative values, counting\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>BFS or Dijkstra\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fewest or cheapest moves\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Does every move cost the same?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>BFS, O(V + E)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Dijkstra if costs &gt;= 0; 0-1 BFS for costs 0 and 1; Bellman-Ford for negatives\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Greedy or DP\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Optimise over a sequence of choices\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Can you prove one choice is always safe, and does a brute force fail to break it?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Greedy, usually sort plus one pass\u003c/td>\u003ctd>DP over the choices\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Heap, sort or quickselect\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Smallest, largest, top k\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Does data arrive or change between questions?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Heap: O(log n) per operation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fixed data: sort for full order, heapq.nsmallest or quickselect for top k\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Backtracking or DP\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Choices, \"ways\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Must every solution be listed, or only counted or optimised?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>List them: backtracking (the output can be exponential)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Count or best, with repeating states: DP\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Union-find or BFS\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Connectivity\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Are links only added and is the relation symmetric, with no paths or distances needed?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Union-find, near O(1) per operation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>BFS or DFS: directed reach, distances, paths\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Two problems from this module's review sets show the first pair from both sides: 1004 (longest run of 1s with at most k flips) is a window, because a stretch with too many zeros stays bad as it grows; 523 (a stretch whose sum is a multiple of k) is prefix sums, because \"multiple of k\" is not monotone at all. And 2101 (bombs triggering bombs) looks like union-find but is not: a large bomb can reach a small one that cannot reach back, so the relation is not symmetric.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Settle it with a counterexample hunt\u003c/h5>\u003cp>When the deciding question is hard to answer by thought alone, test it. Write the candidate (fast, maybe wrong), an obviously correct brute force, and a generator of small random inputs; run thousands of cases and stop at the first disagreement. Sort the cases so the smallest failing input is found first: a three-element counterexample explains itself, a fifty-element one does not.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A hunt costs about five minutes to write the brute force, two for the generator, and seconds to run 10,000 small cases. A wrong submission or a wrong interview answer costs far more. The techniques themselves:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Option\u003c/th>\u003cth>Time\u003c/th>\u003cth>Extra space\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Window / prefix sums with a map\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n) / O(n)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(1) or O(alphabet) / O(n)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>BFS / Dijkstra with heapq\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(V + E) / O((V + E) log V)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(V) / O(V + E)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Top k of n by sort / nsmallest / quickselect\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n log n) / O(n log k) / O(n) average\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n) / O(k) / O(1) in place\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Ask the deciding question whenever two atlas rows match. Run a counterexample hunt whenever your idea is greedy, or a window over values that might be negative, or any \"this feels right\" step you cannot prove in two sentences. Skip the hunt when a short proof exists: an exchange argument you can state is better evidence than any number of random tests.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The window idea for \"shortest stretch with sum at least k\" on \u003ccode>[1, -1, 1, 3]\u003c/code> with k = 4. The correct answer is 2 (\u003ccode>[1, 3]\u003c/code>).\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">right = 0: add 1, sum = 1 &lt; 4, nothing to record\u003c/p>\u003cpre>nums   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>  -1   1   3      window [0, 0], sum 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">right = 1: add -1, sum = 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>nums   1  \u003cmark>-1\u003c/mark>   1   3      window [0, 1], sum 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">right = 2: add 1, sum = 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>nums   1  -1  \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>   3       window [0, 2], sum 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">right = 3: add 3, sum = 4 &gt;= 4: record length 4, then drop nums[0] = 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>nums   1  -1   1  \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>       window [0, 3], sum 4, best = 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">sum is now 3 &lt; 4, so the window stops shrinking and the loop ends\u003c/p>\u003cpre>nums   .  \u003cmark>-1\u003c/mark>   1   3      window [1, 3], sum 3, best = 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">But dropping the -1 would have raised the sum back to 4 with length 2: the window answered {{4}}, the truth is {{2}}\u003c/p>\u003cpre>nums   .   .  \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>    [1, 3] sums to 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The random hunt below finds an even smaller case on its own: \u003ccode>([-3, 3], 1)\u003c/code>, where the window never becomes valid and answers -1 although \u003ccode>[3]\u003c/code> alone works.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import math\nimport random\n\n\ndef find_counterexample(candidate, oracle, gen, trials=10_000, seed=0):\n    \"\"\"Compare a fast idea with a slow, obviously correct oracle on random small inputs.\n    Returns the smallest disagreeing input found, or None.\"\"\"\n    rnd = random.Random(seed)\n    cases = sorted((gen(rnd) for _ in range(trials)), key=lambda c: len(repr(c)))\n    for case in cases:\n        if candidate(*case) != oracle(*case):\n            return case\n    return None\n\n\ndef shortest_at_least_window(nums, k):\n    \"\"\"Window idea: valid only when every value is non-negative.\"\"\"\n    best, s, left = math.inf, 0, 0\n    for right, x in enumerate(nums):\n        s += x\n        while s &gt;= k:\n            best = min(best, right - left + 1)\n            s -= nums[left]\n            left += 1\n    return best if best &lt; math.inf else -1\n\n\ndef shortest_at_least_brute(nums, k):\n    best = math.inf\n    for i in range(len(nums)):\n        s = 0\n        for j in range(i, len(nums)):\n            s += nums[j]\n            if s &gt;= k:\n                best = min(best, j - i + 1)\n                break\n    return best if best &lt; math.inf else -1\n\n\ndef gen_signed(rnd):\n    return ([rnd.randint(-3, 4) for _ in range(rnd.randint(1, 6))], rnd.randint(1, 6))\n\n\nprint(find_counterexample(shortest_at_least_window, shortest_at_least_brute, gen_signed))\n# ([-3, 3], 1); with values drawn from 0..4 instead it prints None\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The same harness compares a greedy coin-change function with a DP one: on random coin systems that include 1 it reports \u003ccode>((1, 4, 5), 8)\u003c/code>, where greedy pays 5 + 1 + 1 + 1 (four coins) and DP pays 4 + 4 (two).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Trusting the examples.\u003c/b> Statement examples are chosen to explain, not to break; they rarely contain the negative value or the tie that kills a wrong idea.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Union-find on a directed relation.\u003c/b> It merges both ways, so it answers \"same group\" for pairs that only reach one way.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>BFS with almost equal weights.\u003c/b> Costs of 1 and 2 are not equal costs; use Dijkstra, or split each cost-2 edge into two cost-1 edges.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>A hunt with a buggy oracle.\u003c/b> Check the brute force on the statement's examples first, or the hunt will \"find\" correct code wrong.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">\"Longest stretch whose sum is at most k, all values non-negative\": window or prefix sums? Give the reason in one sentence.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A window: with non-negative values, removing an element from a valid stretch keeps the sum at most k, and adding to an invalid stretch keeps it above k, so validity is monotone and each end only moves forward.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Friendships are added one at a time. After each addition you must answer either (a) \"are these two people connected?\" or (b) \"how many links separate them?\". Which structure for each?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>(a) union-find: links are only added, the relation is symmetric and only membership is asked, at near O(1) per operation. (b) BFS: union-find stores no paths or distances, so you need a traversal of the graph (from one endpoint, O(V + E) per question).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Someone finds the median of a fixed list by pushing every element onto a heap and popping n/2 times. Is it correct, what does it cost, and what would you use instead?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It is correct and costs O(n log n), no better than sorting. For one median of fixed data, sort once (\u003ccode>sorted(a)[len(a) // 2]\u003c/code>) or use quickselect for O(n) on average; the two-heap method earns its keep only when values arrive over time.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>1004 Max Consecutive Ones III.\u003c/b> Before coding, answer the window-or-prefix-sums question in writing. Expected: 4 for \u003ccode>nums = [1, 0, 1, 1, 0, 1, 0], k = 1\u003c/code>, and 0 for \u003ccode>[0, 0, 0], k = 0\u003c/code>; target O(n), O(1) space.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>523 Continuous Subarray Sum.\u003c/b> Same question first. Expected: true for \u003ccode>[2, 4, 1], k = 7\u003c/code>, false for \u003ccode>[3, 1], k = 5\u003c/code>, true for \u003ccode>[5, 0, 0], k = 3\u003c/code> (the two zeros form a valid stretch).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>646 Maximum Length of Pair Chain.\u003c/b> Write a greedy version and an O(n^2) DP version, then run \u003ccode>find_counterexample\u003c/code> between them. Expected: 2 for \u003ccode>[[1, 3], [2, 4], [5, 6], [4, 5]]\u003c/code>, 3 for \u003ccode>[[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]]\u003c/code>, and the hunt returns None.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know the deciding question for six look-alike pairs and how to settle a doubtful idea with a counterexample hunt.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:mixed/how-does-the-same-problem-change-at-n-20-10-3-10":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>The constraints are part of the problem: they tell you which algorithm the setter wants. The same story needs brute force at n = 20, a quadratic or simple DP at 10^3, an O(n log n) or O(n) idea at 10^5, and at 10^9 something that never touches every item, such as a formula, a binary search or fast exponentiation. Read n (and the value range, and the number of queries) before you design anything.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Delivering 5 parcels, you can try every route. Delivering 5,000, you need a rule such as \"nearest next\". Delivering to every house in a country, you no longer plan routes at all; you plan depots and use averages. The task did not change, its size did, and each size has its own tools.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>One story at four sizes\u003c/h5>\u003cp>A robot climbs n steps, moving 1, 2 or 3 steps at a time. How many different routes reach the top, modulo 1,000,000,007? Let f(n) be the answer: the last move was 1, 2 or 3 steps, so f(n) = f(n - 1) + f(n - 2) + f(n - 3), with f(0) = 1 and f(negative) = 0.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>n\u003c/th>\u003cth>Budget allows\u003c/th>\u003cth>What works\u003c/th>\u003cth>Why the previous version stops working\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>20\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Exponential\u003c/td>\u003ctd>List every route explicitly: 121,415 routes, about 0.2 s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>(nothing yet)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>10^3\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n^2)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A loop over the recurrence\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Listing is hopeless (about 10^264 routes), and memoised recursion is 1,000 calls deep: Python's default recursion limit is 1,000, so it raises RecursionError\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>10^5\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n log n)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The same loop with three rolling variables, taking \u003ccode>% MOD\u003c/code> every step\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Without the modulus the exact count has 26,465 digits and every addition gets slower\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>10^9\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(log n)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Raise the 3 x 3 step matrix to the n-th power by repeated squaring: about 30 squarings\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The O(n) loop managed 10^7 steps in 2.7 s, so 10^9 would take about 4.5 minutes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch5>Other stories, the same lesson\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Story\u003c/th>\u003cth>n = 20\u003c/th>\u003cth>n = 10^3\u003c/th>\u003cth>n = 10^5\u003c/th>\u003cth>n = 10^9\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Count pairs i &lt; j with a[i] + a[j] &lt;= T\u003c/td>\u003ctd>All pairs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>All pairs (5·10^5)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Sort, then two pointers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The values are 1..n: count by arithmetic, O(1)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Can a subset reach the sum T?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Try all 2^20 subsets\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Subset-sum DP if T &lt;= 10^4 (O(nT))\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Only with special structure; in general it is NP-hard\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Values 1..n: every T from 0 to n(n + 1)/2 is reachable\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The second table carries a warning: when a problem that is hard in general appears with a large n, the constraints are hiding structure (small values, a special sequence) that you are meant to find.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Counting the work for the climbing story: listing routes costs one step per route, about 1.84^n; the loop costs n iterations of three additions; the matrix method costs one or two 3 x 3 multiplications (27 multiply-adds each) per bit of n, so about 2 · 30 · 27 = 1,620 operations at n = 10^9. Same answer, from minutes down to under a millisecond.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Always, as the first two minutes of a problem: n, the value range, the number of queries q, and the time limit. Check the product of the sizes, not each one alone: n = 10^5 with q = 10^5 queries rules out O(n) per query. When the constraints are tiny, enjoy it: a clean brute force is the intended solution, not a lack of ambition.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003ccode>routes_matrix(10)\u003c/code> processes the bits of 10 = 0b1010 from the lowest, keeping \u003ccode>result\u003c/code> (the product so far) and \u003ccode>step\u003c/code> (the matrix squared once per bit).\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start: result = M^0 (the identity), step = M^1; bits of 10 from the right: 0, 1, 0, 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>n = 1010 (binary)   result = M^0   step = M^1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Bit 0 is {{0}}: leave result, square step\u003c/p>\u003cpre>n = 101             result = M^0   step = \u003cmark>M^2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Bit 1 is {{1}}: result = result * step, then square step\u003c/p>\u003cpre>n = 10              result = \u003cmark>M^2\u003c/mark>   step = \u003cmark>M^4\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Bit 2 is {{0}}: square step only\u003c/p>\u003cpre>n = 1               result = M^2   step = \u003cmark>M^8\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Bit 3 is {{1}}: result = M^2 * M^8\u003c/p>\u003cpre>n = 0               result = \u003cmark>M^10\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">f(10) is the top-left entry of M^10: {{274}}, matching 1, 1, 2, 4, 7, 13, 24, 44, 81, 149, 274\u003c/p>\u003cpre>f(0..10) = 1 1 2 4 7 13 24 44 81 149 \u003cmark>274\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>MOD = 1_000_000_007\n\n\ndef routes_loop(n):\n    \"\"\"n up to about 10^6: bottom-up with three rolling variables. O(n) time, O(1) space.\"\"\"\n    a, b, c = 0, 0, 1                     # f(i - 3), f(i - 2), f(i - 1), starting from f(0) = 1\n    for _ in range(n):\n        a, b, c = b, c, (a + b + c) % MOD\n    return c\n\n\ndef mat_mult(X, Y):\n    return [[sum(X[i][t] * Y[t][j] for t in range(3)) % MOD for j in range(3)] for i in range(3)]\n\n\ndef routes_matrix(n):\n    \"\"\"n up to 10^18: (f(i), f(i-1), f(i-2)) = M (f(i-1), f(i-2), f(i-3)), so apply M^n to\n    (f(0), f(-1), f(-2)) = (1, 0, 0). O(log n) matrix products.\"\"\"\n    result = [[1, 0, 0], [0, 1, 0], [0, 0, 1]]\n    step = [[1, 1, 1], [1, 0, 0], [0, 1, 0]]\n    while n:\n        if n &amp; 1:\n            result = mat_mult(result, step)\n        step = mat_mult(step, step)\n        n &gt;&gt;= 1\n    return result[0][0]\n\n\nassert routes_loop(10) == routes_matrix(10) == 274\nassert routes_loop(10**5) == routes_matrix(10**5)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>The recursion limit.\u003c/b> Memoised recursion is an O(n) algorithm that still crashes at n = 1,000 in Python; go bottom-up, or raise the limit only with care.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Forgetting the modulus.\u003c/b> Python's integers never overflow, so nothing warns you; the numbers just grow until every operation is slow. In C++ or Java they overflow silently.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Reading \"values up to 10^9\" as \"n up to 10^9\".\u003c/b> A large value range forbids arrays indexed by value (use a dict or coordinate compression) but says nothing about n.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Ignoring the number of test cases.\u003c/b> \"Up to 10^4 test cases, sum of n up to 2·10^5\" is a different budget from \"10^4 test cases of n = 2·10^5\".\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">\u003ccode>routes_loop(10**7)\u003c/code> took 2.7 s. Roughly how long would \u003ccode>routes_loop(10**8)\u003c/code> take, and what does that tell you about n = 10^8 in Python?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>About 27 s, ten times as long, because the loop is linear. Even an O(n) loop of simple Python operations is too slow around 10^8; you need either C-level built-ins (or NumPy) or an algorithm below O(n).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does the memoised version fail at n = 1,000 but succeed at n = 600?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each pending call f(i) waits on f(i - 1), so the call stack grows to about n frames before anything returns. Python's default recursion limit is 1,000 frames, so n = 600 fits and n = 1,000 raises RecursionError, although the algorithm itself is fine.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A problem has n &lt;= 300 and asks for something you can do in O(n^3) or, with a clever trick, in O(n). Which do you write first?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The O(n^3) version if it is simpler: 2.7·10^7 simple operations is a few seconds in Python, so check whether the inner loop can be cut to O(n^2) (9·10^4 operations, instant). With n this small the setter expects a cubic or quadratic idea; the O(n) trick is a bonus, not the target.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Write down the target complexity from each problem's constraints before choosing an idea.\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>1442 Count Triplets That Can Form Two Arrays of Equal XOR\u003c/b> (n &lt;= 300). Expected: 2 for \u003ccode>[2, 2, 2]\u003c/code> and 2 for \u003ccode>[1, 3, 2]\u003c/code>; an O(n^2) solution is comfortably within the budget.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>1823 Find the Winner of the Circular Game\u003c/b> (n &lt;= 500). Solve it once by simulation and once in O(n). Expected: 5 for \u003ccode>n = 6, k = 2\u003c/code>, and 4 for \u003ccode>n = 4, k = 1\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>2101 Detonate the Maximum Bombs\u003c/b> (n &lt;= 100). Expected: 2 for \u003ccode>[[0, 0, 2], [3, 0, 1], [1, 0, 1]]\u003c/code>; the constraint permits O(n^3).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to read the constraints first and match the algorithm to the size, including when n is too large to touch every item.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:mixed/when-should-you-re-solve-a-problem-and-how-does-":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Solving a problem once proves you could do it that day. Re-solving it \u003cb>from a blank file\u003c/b> after a gap, then after longer gaps (a few days, a week, three weeks, two months), makes it something you can do months later under pressure. Grade every attempt honestly: \u003cb>Solved\u003c/b> only if you wrote the brute force and the optimal solution yourself; \u003cb>Brute only\u003c/b> if you reached a correct but slow solution or needed a nudge; \u003cb>Stuck\u003c/b> if you needed the key insight or the solution. Brute only and Stuck come back in Today's session three days later; the longer rungs are yours to schedule.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Memory fades fastest just after learning. Pulling an idea back out when it has started to fade (but is not gone) strengthens it far more than rereading it while it is fresh: this is the testing effect combined with spacing. One successful re-solve after two months is worth more than three re-solves in the same week, and a failed re-solve tells you something a fresh solve cannot: which part you only understood while it was in front of you.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>The ladder\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Rung\u003c/th>\u003cth>Gap after the last attempt\u003c/th>\u003cth>Who schedules it\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>0\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3 days, after Brute only or Stuck\u003c/td>\u003ctd>This page: Today's session\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>1\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1 week, after a clean solve\u003c/td>\u003ctd>You: your journal\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3 weeks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>You\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>3\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2 months\u003c/td>\u003ctd>You\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Off\u003c/td>\u003ctd>(none)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The problem retires to the bank pool\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The rule is one line: a clean solve climbs one rung; anything else drops back to rung 0. The gaps expand because, as Cepeda's data shows, the best gap is a sizeable fraction of how long you want to remember, so each successful retrieval earns a longer one.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>The blank-file rule\u003c/h5>\u003cp>A re-solve starts from an empty file with the statement only: no old code, no notes. Keep the page's timebox (Easy 20, Medium 30, Hard 45 minutes). Write the brute force first, then the optimal version, then test both against each other on small random inputs. Retyping code you remember is not a re-solve; it tests your typing, not your reasoning.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Grading honestly\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>What happened\u003c/th>\u003cth>Mark\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Brute force and optimal written alone, within about 1.5x the timebox; you can state the cost and why it is correct\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Solved\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Only a correct slow solution, or you needed \"What to notice\" or \"A nudge\", or you went far over the timebox\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Brute only\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>You needed \"Key insight\" or read a solution\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Stuck\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch5>How Today's session runs rung 0\u003c/h5>\u003cul>\u003cli>Marking \u003cb>Brute only\u003c/b> or \u003cb>Stuck\u003c/b> records the time; three days later the item becomes due.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The review slot holds one item per session: the oldest due item; if nothing is due, the next problem of the earliest open review set.\u003c/li>\u003cli>After the re-solve, mark the new result. \u003cb>Solved\u003c/b> takes it out of the queue. If the result is the same status as before, clear it and set it again so the date updates (clicking a selected status clears it).\u003c/li>\u003cli>The \u003cb>Review\u003c/b> view lists everything currently marked Brute only or Stuck, for a longer catch-up session.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The page does not schedule rungs 1 to 3; the template computes those dates for your journal.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Derive the weekly load before you commit to it. If you add N new problems a week and each climbs r rungs, then once the pipeline is full you face about N x r re-solves a week. With N = 5 and all four rungs that is 20 re-solves a week; at 15 to 20 minutes each (re-solves are faster than first solves), five to seven hours, which is too much. So be selective: give every Brute only or Stuck problem the full ladder, give clean first solves only rungs 1 and 3, and cap reviews at about a third of your practice time. That brings N = 5 down to roughly 8 to 12 re-solves a week.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use the full ladder for core problems of each module, for anything you marked Brute only or Stuck, and for interview staples. Skip it for bank problems that taught you nothing new, and for problems you can only \"solve\" by remembering code: rewrite their cards instead, then re-solve. If the queue grows beyond one or two re-solves per session, stop adding new problems for a few days; review debt only grows.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>One problem's history through the ladder (dates from the template below).\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">2 Nov: first attempt, needed the key insight: {{Stuck}}. Rung 0: due in 3 days\u003c/p>\u003cpre>history: Stuck                         next: \u003cmark>5 Nov\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">5 Nov (Today's session offers it): reached only the slow version: {{Brute only}}. Back to rung 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>history: Stuck, Brute                  next: \u003cmark>8 Nov\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">8 Nov: clean solve from a blank file: {{Solved}}. Rung 1: one week\u003c/p>\u003cpre>history: Stuck, Brute, Solved          next: \u003cmark>15 Nov\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">15 Nov: clean again. Rung 2: three weeks\u003c/p>\u003cpre>history: ..., Solved, Solved           next: \u003cmark>6 Dec\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">6 Dec: clean again. Rung 3: two months\u003c/p>\u003cpre>history: ..., Solved x3                next: \u003cmark>4 Feb\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">4 Feb: clean after two months: off the ladder\u003c/p>\u003cpre>history: ..., Solved x4                next: \u003cmark>none\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from datetime import date, timedelta\n\nLADDER = [3, 7, 21, 60]          # days until the next re-solve, by rung\n\n\ndef next_resolve(history):\n    \"\"\"history: [(date, result)] oldest first, result in {\"solved\", \"brute\", \"stuck\"}.\n    A clean solve climbs one rung; anything else drops to rung 0 (a re-solve in 3 days).\n    Returns the next re-solve date, or None once the problem has climbed off the ladder.\"\"\"\n    rung = 0\n    for _, result in history:\n        rung = rung + 1 if result == \"solved\" else 0\n    if rung &gt;= len(LADDER):\n        return None\n    return history[-1][0] + timedelta(days=LADDER[rung])\n\n\nh = [(date(2026, 11, 2), \"stuck\"), (date(2026, 11, 5), \"brute\"), (date(2026, 11, 8), \"solved\")]\nprint(next_resolve(h))                                  # 2026-11-15\nprint(next_resolve([(date(2026, 11, 2), \"solved\")]))    # 2026-11-09: a clean first solve starts at rung 1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Retyping instead of re-deriving.\u003c/b> If you remember the code, write the brute force first anyway, and say the invariant aloud before the optimal version.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Re-solving the same day.\u003c/b> It feels productive and measures short-term memory only.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Generous grading.\u003c/b> A Solved mark removes the problem from review; if you hesitated at the key step, it was not Solved.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Letting the queue grow.\u003c/b> One or two re-solves per session is sustainable; ten is a backlog you will skip.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Re-solving favourites.\u003c/b> The problems you dislike are usually the ones that need it.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A history reads \u003ccode>[(1 Mar, solved), (8 Mar, stuck)]\u003c/code>. When is the next re-solve, and why not later?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>11 Mar. The Stuck result drops the problem back to rung 0, three days after the failed attempt; the earlier clean solve does not count any more, because the failure shows the idea had not stuck.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why do the gaps grow (3, 7, 21, 60 days) instead of staying at one week?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each successful retrieval makes the memory more durable, and the best gap is a fraction of how long you want to remember (Cepeda and colleagues: roughly 20-40% of a one-week horizon, 5-10% of a one-year horizon). Fixed short gaps spend time re-proving what you already hold; growing gaps test you near the point of forgetting, which is where retrieval helps most.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">You re-solve 946 cleanly but it takes 50 minutes against a 30-minute timebox. What do you mark, and what happens next?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Brute only: past about 1.5x the timebox the solution is not yet fluent, even if it is correct. Today's session brings it back three days later; once a re-solve is clean and on time, mark Solved and put the one-week date in your journal.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Solve each problem today, record the attempt as \u003ccode>(date, result)\u003c/code>, and compute its next date with \u003ccode>next_resolve\u003c/code>. Expected: three dates in your journal (three days out for Brute only or Stuck, one week out for a clean solve).\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>946 Validate Stack Sequences.\u003c/b> Expected: false for \u003ccode>pushed = [1, 2, 3], popped = [3, 1, 2]\u003c/code> and true for \u003ccode>popped = [2, 3, 1]\u003c/code>; O(n).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>240 Search a 2D Matrix II.\u003c/b> Expected: true for target 6 and false for target 10 in \u003ccode>[[1, 4, 7], [2, 5, 8], [3, 6, 9]]\u003c/code>; O(m + n).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>328 Odd Even Linked List.\u003c/b> Expected: 1 3 5 2 4 6 from the list 1 to 6, and a single node 7 unchanged; O(1) extra space.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to run a spaced re-solve ladder from a blank file, grade attempts honestly, and combine it with Today's session.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:mixed/how-do-you-keep-an-error-log-that-actually-reduc":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A problem journal records what you learnt; an \u003cb>error log\u003c/b> records how you fail. One line per mistake: date, problem, \u003cb>category\u003c/b>, what happened. Categories (misread, wrong technique, broken invariant, off-by-one, complexity, missed edge case, Python cost trap) let you review by kind of mistake, so one drill fixes a whole class of future bugs instead of one problem.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Aviation checklists and blameless post-mortems exist because people repeat the same few failure modes. A post-mortem does not ask \"who was careless?\" but \"which check would have caught this?\". Your log asks the same question of your code, and after a month the answer is usually one or two categories that account for most of your losses.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>The categories\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Category\u003c/th>\u003cth>Typical symptom\u003c/th>\u003cth>Drill that targets it\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>misread\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Solved a different problem; missed a constraint\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Restate the problem in one sentence and list the constraints before coding\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>technique\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Right code for the wrong idea\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Two classification sprints; add the missed cue to your atlas\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>invariant\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Pointers or windows drift; wrong on some inputs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Write the invariant as a comment first, then assert it while testing\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>off-by-one\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fails at the boundaries; empty or size-one inputs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Half-open ranges; test sizes 0, 1, 2 on purpose\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>complexity\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Correct but times out\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Compute the target from n before designing; time a worst-case input\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>edge-case\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fails on duplicates, negatives, zeros, extremes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Run the edge-case catalogue before submitting\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>python-cost\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hidden O(n) inside a loop\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Check the cost sheet: \u003ccode>list.pop(0)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>x in list\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>s +=\u003c/code> in loops, slicing\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch5>The line and the weekly review\u003c/h5>\u003cp>One line, written right after the attempt: \u003ccode>2026-11-04 | 930 | off-by-one | did not seed the counts with prefix 0\u003c/code>. Once a week, count by category, take the largest, and apply its drill to every problem for the next week. At the next review, check whether that category's count fell. Keep the categories few: seven is enough to separate the causes without agonising over the label.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>About 30 seconds per entry and ten minutes per weekly review. Over a month that is under an hour, and it typically removes the single most expensive habit you have (often off-by-one or skipping the constraints), which costs far more than an hour.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Log every Brute only or Stuck result and every wrong submission, including the ones you fixed in two minutes: the cheap mistakes recur most. Do not log typos that the interpreter flagged instantly unless the same typo keeps coming back. When a mistake fits two categories, choose the earliest step of the solving loop that went wrong (a misread that led to the wrong technique is a misread).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Turning a failed attempt into one log line.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">1283 fails a hidden test: the answer is too small by one\u003c/p>\u003cpre>symptom: \u003cmark>wrong answer\u003c/mark>, off by one\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Reproduce with the smallest input that fails, found with a brute force\u003c/p>\u003cpre>nums = [4, 4], threshold = 2   expected 4, got \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Find the line: the check summed x // d, which rounds down; the statement rounds each quotient up\u003c/p>\u003cpre>sum(x // d)  \u003cmark>should be sum((x + d - 1) // d)\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Which check would have caught it? Restating the statement in my own words: \"each quotient rounded up\"\u003c/p>\u003cpre>category: \u003cmark>misread\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Write the line\u003c/p>\u003cpre>2026-11-06 | 1283 | misread | rounded quotients down, not up\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">At the weekly review off-by-one has three entries, the most: next week's drill is {{test sizes 0, 1, 2 on purpose}}\u003c/p>\u003cpre>off-by-one 3   technique 2   edge-case 1   ...\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import defaultdict\n\nDRILLS = {\n    \"misread\": \"Restate the problem in one sentence and list the constraints before coding.\",\n    \"technique\": \"Do two classification sprints; add the missed cue to your atlas.\",\n    \"invariant\": \"Write the invariant as a comment first, then assert it while testing.\",\n    \"off-by-one\": \"Use half-open ranges; test sizes 0, 1, 2 on purpose.\",\n    \"complexity\": \"Compute the target from n before designing; time a worst-case input.\",\n    \"edge-case\": \"Run the edge-case catalogue: empty, one, duplicates, negatives, extremes.\",\n    \"python-cost\": \"Check the cost sheet: list.pop(0), x in list, s += in loops, slicing.\",\n}\n\n\ndef review(log_lines, top=3):\n    \"\"\"log_lines: 'date | problem | category | what happened'. Returns the commonest categories,\n    each with its count, its drill and the problems that showed it.\"\"\"\n    by_cat = defaultdict(list)\n    for line in log_lines:\n        day, problem, category, note = [part.strip() for part in line.split(\"|\", 3)]\n        by_cat[category].append(problem)\n    ranked = sorted(by_cat.items(), key=lambda kv: -len(kv[1]))[:top]\n    return [(cat, len(probs), DRILLS.get(cat, \"new category: define a drill\"), probs)\n            for cat, probs in ranked]\n\n\nLOG = \"\"\"2026-11-02 | 1004 | technique | tried prefix sums; the window was enough\n2026-11-03 | 523 | edge-case | forgot that the stretch needs length &gt;= 2\n2026-11-04 | 930 | off-by-one | did not seed the counts with prefix 0\n2026-11-06 | 1283 | misread | rounded the quotients down; the statement rounds up\n2026-11-07 | 2300 | complexity | nested loop, 10^10 pairs\n2026-11-09 | 1944 | technique | reached for a heap; a stack was the idea\n2026-11-10 | 1209 | python-cost | rebuilt the string with += inside the loop\n2026-11-12 | 1248 | off-by-one | atMost(k - 1) computed with the wrong window bound\n2026-11-13 | 2125 | off-by-one | skipped the last non-empty row\"\"\".splitlines()\nprint(review(LOG)[0])\n# ('off-by-one', 3, 'Use half-open ranges; test sizes 0, 1, 2 on purpose.', ['930', '1248', '2125'])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Vague categories.\u003c/b> \"Bug\" or \"silly mistake\" cannot be drilled. Name the check that would have caught it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Logging only disasters.\u003c/b> The two-minute fixes are the most frequent and the cheapest to eliminate.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Never reviewing.\u003c/b> A log nobody reads is a diary. Put the weekly review in your calendar.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Too many categories.\u003c/b> Twenty categories split the counts so thin that nothing stands out.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Blame instead of process.\u003c/b> \"I was careless\" changes nothing; \"I skip the size-one test\" leads to a drill.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your solution passed every example but timed out on n = 10^5 because you used \u003ccode>nums.pop(0)\u003c/code> inside a loop. Which category, and why not \"complexity\"?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>python-cost. The algorithm was linear on paper; the hidden O(n) cost of \u003ccode>list.pop(0)\u003c/code> (every element shifts left) made it quadratic. The drill is the Python cost sheet (use \u003ccode>collections.deque.popleft\u003c/code>), not a new algorithm.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why review by category rather than re-reading each problem's notes?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Mistakes cluster by kind, not by problem. Ten off-by-one errors in ten different problems look unrelated problem by problem, but by category they become one habit with one drill (half-open ranges, size 0-2 tests) that prevents the next ten as well.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A log line reads \u003ccode>1004 | bug | forgot something\u003c/code>. What is wrong with it, and how would you rewrite it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Neither the category nor the note says which check failed, so it cannot be counted or drilled. Rewrite it with a real category and a precise note, for example \u003ccode>2026-11-02 | 1004 | invariant | moved left before decrementing the zero count\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Solve the three problems and log every slip, including ones your own tests caught. Then run \u003ccode>review\u003c/code> on your lines. Expected: either at least one category with two or more entries, or an empty log you can honestly defend.\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>1283 Find the Smallest Divisor Given a Threshold.\u003c/b> Expected: 6 for \u003ccode>nums = [2, 7, 11], threshold = 5\u003c/code>, and 4 for \u003ccode>[4, 4], threshold = 2\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>1248 Count Number of Nice Subarrays.\u003c/b> Expected: 4 for \u003ccode>[1, 2, 1, 2, 1], k = 2\u003c/code>, and 0 for \u003ccode>[2, 4], k = 1\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>2300 Successful Pairs of Spells and Potions.\u003c/b> Expected: \u003ccode>[1, 3]\u003c/code> for \u003ccode>spells = [2, 5], potions = [1, 2, 3, 4], success = 8\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to log mistakes by category and turn the most frequent one into a drill that prevents a whole class of bugs.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:mixed/what-belongs-on-a-dsa-flashcard-and-what-does-no":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A good card asks for \u003cb>one fact you need to rebuild a solution\u003c/b>: the cue that points to a technique, the invariant that makes it correct, the recurrence, the cost with its reason, or the bug to avoid. A card holding a whole solution trains you to recognise code you have seen, not to produce it. Write four or five cards per solved problem from your journal, review them for a few minutes a day, and let a scheduler space them out.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A card is an interview question you can answer in one breath: \"why is the monotonic stack linear?\", \"what must the prefix-count map start with?\". Problems test whether you can build a solution; cards keep the parts sharp between problems, the way scales keep a pianist's fingers ready for pieces.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Five kinds of card\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Kind\u003c/th>\u003cth>Front\u003c/th>\u003cth>Back\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Cue to technique\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Count subarrays with an exact sum, values may be negative: which idea?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Prefix sums with a count map\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Invariant\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Monotonic stack for next greater: what is true of the stack?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Values strictly decrease from bottom to top, and every element on it is still waiting for its answer\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Recurrence\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fewest coins for amount a: recurrence and base case?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>best[a] = 1 + min(best[a - c]) over coins c &lt;= a; best[0] = 0\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Cost with reason\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Why is the monotonic stack O(n) overall?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Each index is pushed once and popped at most once\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Pitfall\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Prefix-count map: what must it contain before the loop?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>{0: 1}, so stretches that start at index 0 are counted\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch5>What makes a card bad\u003c/h5>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Whole solutions.\u003c/b> \"Solve 1209\" with twenty lines of code on the back: you will recognise the code and still fail to write it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Vague fronts.\u003c/b> \"What is dynamic programming?\" has no checkable answer, so you cannot grade yourself honestly.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Two facts at once.\u003c/b> \"Cost and invariant of a monotonic stack\" gets graded as right when you knew one of them. Split it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Facts you never understood.\u003c/b> A card cannot create understanding; work the problem first, then write the card.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch5>Scheduling\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Use a spaced-repetition app such as Anki (its scheduler is based on SuperMemo's SM-2 algorithm, or the newer FSRS), or a Leitner box: cards move up a box each time you answer correctly and back to the first box when you miss, and higher boxes are reviewed less often. Anki imports plain text files with one card per line and fields separated by tabs, which the template below produces.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Say you solve five problems a week and write four cards each: 20 new cards a week. With gaps that roughly double after each correct answer, a card costs about six to eight reviews in its first year, so each week's batch costs about 140 reviews spread over the year. In steady state that is about 20 reviews a day; at roughly ten seconds each, three to four minutes a day.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Write cards right after the journal entry for a solved problem, and from each module's \"Spot it\" cues. Review them as a warm-up at the start of a session. Do not let cards replace problems: cards keep the parts ready, but only re-solving from a blank file proves you can assemble them.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Turning one journal entry (for 930, counting 0/1 subarrays with a given sum) into cards with the template.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The journal entry: problem, cue, technique, invariant, cost, pitfall\u003c/p>\u003cpre>cue: count subarrays with an exact sum, zeros allowed\ntechnique: prefix sums with a count map\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Card 1, cue to technique: the front is the {{cue}}, the back names the idea\u003c/p>\u003cpre>count subarrays with an exact sum, zeros allowed: which idea?\n-&gt; \u003cmark>prefix sums with a count map\u003c/mark> (930 Binary Subarrays With Sum)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Card 2, invariant: one sentence that must hold at every step\u003c/p>\u003cpre>prefix sums with a count map: what stays true at every step?\n-&gt; seen[s] = number of \u003cmark>earlier\u003c/mark> prefixes equal to s\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Card 3, cost with its reason\u003c/p>\u003cpre>-&gt; \u003cmark>O(n)\u003c/mark>: one pass, O(1) expected per dict operation\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Card 4, the pitfall\u003c/p>\u003cpre>930: the bug to avoid?  -&gt; \u003cmark>seed the map\u003c/mark>: seen = {0: 1}\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Export: one line per card, a tab between front and back, ready for Anki's text import\u003c/p>\u003cpre>front &lt;TAB&gt; back\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def cards_from_entry(entry):\n    \"\"\"entry: dict with problem, cue, technique, invariant, cost, pitfall.\n    Returns (front, back) pairs, one fact each. Never the whole solution.\"\"\"\n    p = entry[\"problem\"]\n    return [\n        (f\"{entry['cue']}: which idea?\", f\"{entry['technique']} ({p})\"),\n        (f\"{entry['technique']}: what stays true at every step?\", entry[\"invariant\"]),\n        (f\"{entry['technique']} on {p}: target cost and why?\", entry[\"cost\"]),\n        (f\"{p}: the bug to avoid?\", entry[\"pitfall\"]),\n    ]\n\n\ndef to_tsv(cards):\n    \"\"\"Tab-separated front/back lines, which Anki's plain-text importer reads.\"\"\"\n    clean = lambda s: s.replace(\"\\t\", \" \").replace(\"\\n\", \" \")\n    return \"\\n\".join(f\"{clean(f)}\\t{clean(b)}\" for f, b in cards)\n\n\nentry = dict(problem=\"930 Binary Subarrays With Sum\",\n             cue=\"count subarrays with an exact sum, zeros allowed\",\n             technique=\"prefix sums with a count map\",\n             invariant=\"seen[s] = number of earlier prefixes equal to s, before adding the current prefix\",\n             cost=\"O(n): one pass, O(1) expected per dict operation\",\n             pitfall=\"start with seen = {0: 1} so subarrays that begin at index 0 count\")\nopen(\"cards.tsv\", \"w\").write(to_tsv(cards_from_entry(entry)) + \"\\n\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cards that are too big.\u003c/b> If the answer takes more than one breath, split the card.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Ambiguous fronts.\u003c/b> If two different answers are both right, add context to the front (\"for non-negative values\").\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Reviewing instead of solving.\u003c/b> Cards support practice; they do not replace re-solves.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Keeping bad cards.\u003c/b> A card you keep failing for the same reason is badly written: rewrite or delete it.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Rewrite the card \"Explain the sliding window technique\" as two good cards.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For example: \"Longest stretch with at most k distinct letters: which idea, and what makes it valid?\" with the back \"Variable window; validity is monotone: shrinking a valid window keeps it valid\". And: \"Variable window: why is it O(n) although it has a loop inside a loop?\" with the back \"Each index enters once (right end) and leaves at most once (left end)\". Each asks for one checkable fact.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is putting the solution code for 1209 on a card a poor investment?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Reviewing it trains recognition: you see the code and think \"yes, that one\", which feels like knowing it. Producing a solution needs the cue, the idea (a stack of runs) and the invariant, each retrieved on its own; cards for those transfer to other problems, a code card transfers to nothing.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In \u003ccode>to_tsv\u003c/code>, why are tabs inside a card replaced by spaces?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The importer splits each line on tabs: a tab inside the front or back would create an extra field and shift the text into the wrong place. Newlines are replaced for the same reason, since each line is one card.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Solve each problem, then write four cards for it with the template. Expected: no card longer than two short lines, none containing more than one line of code, and at least one cue card and one invariant card per problem.\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>1209 Remove All Adjacent Duplicates in String II.\u003c/b> Expected: \u003ccode>\"z\"\u003c/code> for \u003ccode>s = \"xyyyxxz\", k = 3\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>\"\"\u003c/code> for \u003ccode>\"aabbaa\", k = 2\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>1019 Next Greater Node In Linked List.\u003c/b> Expected: \u003ccode>[4, 4, 5, 5, 0]\u003c/code> for the list 3, 1, 4, 2, 5.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>16 3Sum Closest.\u003c/b> Expected: 2 for \u003ccode>[-2, 0, 1, 3]\u003c/code> with target 2, and 3 for \u003ccode>[1, 1, 1, 0]\u003c/code> with target 100.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to write small single-fact cards and schedule them, so the parts of every solution stay ready between problems.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:mixed/how-do-you-find-your-weak-spots-from-your-tracke":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Your statuses are data. For each module, the share of tried problems you marked Brute only or Stuck shows where your understanding is thin, and the error log shows why. Pick the worst one or two modules, re-read their cards, redo their Stuck problems from a blank file, and add a few fresh bank problems from them to your practice. Measure again in two weeks.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>You do not optimise a program by speeding up every line; you profile it and fix the hot spot. Practice time works the same way. Spreading effort evenly over thirty modules feels fair, but most of the gain is in the two or three where you keep getting stuck.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>What the page already shows\u003c/h5>\u003cul>\u003cli>Module headers and the rail show solved counts per module; the stage map shows lessons and problems per stage.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The status filter shows only problems with a chosen status, and the \u003cb>Review\u003c/b> view lists everything marked Brute only or Stuck.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The progress menu has \u003cb>Export all progress as a JSON file\u003c/b>. Problem statuses sit under \u003ccode>trackers[\"dsa-status\"]\u003c/code>, each \u003ccode>{\"status\": s, \"updatedAt\": ms}\u003c/code> with 1 Solved, 2 Brute only, 3 Stuck.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch5>Three numbers per module\u003c/h5>\u003cp>For each module count the problems you have \u003cb>tried\u003c/b> (any status), and from them the \u003cb>help rate\u003c/b> (Brute only plus Stuck, divided by tried) and the \u003cb>stuck rate\u003c/b> (Stuck divided by tried).\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Signal\u003c/th>\u003cth>Likely cause\u003c/th>\u003cth>Next two weeks\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>High stuck rate in one module\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The core idea did not land\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Re-read its cards, redo its Stuck problems from a blank file, then three bank problems\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>High help rate in mixed sets, low in module practice\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Recognition, not mechanics\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Classification sprints, the atlas, look-alike pairs\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Mostly Brute only\u003c/td>\u003ctd>You find a solution but not the optimal one\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Target complexity first; the optimisation ladder in the Deriving solutions module\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>One error-log category dominates\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A habit\u003c/td>\u003ctd>That category's drill on every problem\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The export and the analysis take about ten minutes, every two weeks. The script runs in time linear in the number of tracked items, instant even for the full bank of about 900 problems.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Every two weeks, after finishing a stage, and before choosing what to revise for an interview. Do not do it after every problem: with three or four attempts per module, one bad evening looks like a weakness. Require at least three tried problems before you trust a module's rate.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003ccode>weak_spots\u003c/code> on a small export: seven tried problems in two modules.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Load: statuses from the export, and problem -&gt; module from the page's data\u003c/p>\u003cpre>window: 3 1 , 76 2 , 424 1 , 1004 1     prefix: 560 1 , 930 2 , 523 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Tally the window module: four tried, three Solved, one Brute only\u003c/p>\u003cpre>window  tried \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>  solved 3  brute 1  stuck 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Tally the prefix module: three tried, one of each status\u003c/p>\u003cpre>prefix  tried \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>  solved 1  brute 1  stuck 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Help rates: (brute + stuck) / tried\u003c/p>\u003cpre>window  1/4 = \u003cmark>0.25\u003c/mark>      prefix  2/3 = \u003cmark>0.67\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Both modules reach the three-attempt minimum, so rank by help rate\u003c/p>\u003cpre>0.67 Prefix sums ...   0.25 Sliding window ...\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Plan: prefix sums first: re-read the count-map card, redo {{523}} (Stuck) from a blank file, add three bank problems\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import json\nfrom collections import Counter, defaultdict\n\n\ndef weak_spots(status, module_of, names, min_tried=3):\n    \"\"\"status: problem id -&gt; 0..3 (1 Solved, 2 Brute only, 3 Stuck). module_of: id -&gt; module key.\n    Returns (help rate, module, tried, solved, brute only, stuck), worst first.\"\"\"\n    tally = defaultdict(Counter)\n    for pid, s in status.items():\n        if s and pid in module_of:\n            tally[module_of[pid]][s] += 1\n    rows = []\n    for key, c in tally.items():\n        tried = sum(c.values())\n        if tried &gt;= min_tried:\n            rows.append((round((c[2] + c[3]) / tried, 2), names[key], tried, c[1], c[2], c[3]))\n    return sorted(rows, reverse=True)\n\n\ndef load(progress_file_text, page_html_text):\n    \"\"\"progress: the exported JSON file; page: the saved dsa.html, whose data sits in a\n    &lt;script id=\"guide-data\"&gt; element.\"\"\"\n    entries = json.loads(progress_file_text)[\"trackers\"][\"dsa-status\"]\n    status = {pid: e[\"status\"] for pid, e in entries.items()}\n    start = page_html_text.index('id=\"guide-data\"&gt;') + len('id=\"guide-data\"&gt;')\n    data = json.loads(page_html_text[start:page_html_text.index(\"&lt;/script&gt;\", start)])\n    module_of, names = {}, {}\n    for m in data[\"modules\"]:\n        names[m[\"key\"]] = m[\"name\"]\n        for row in m.get(\"practice\", []):\n            module_of[row[0]] = m[\"key\"]\n        for pid in m.get(\"bank\", []):\n            module_of[pid] = m[\"key\"]\n    return status, module_of, names\n\n\n# status, module_of, names = load(open(\"study-progress.json\").read(), open(\"dsa.html\").read())\n# for row in weak_spots(status, module_of, names): print(row)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Card statuses (Done, Needs practice, Revisit) live in the same export under ids that are not problem numbers; \u003ccode>module_of\u003c/code> only maps problems, so cards are ignored here. Read their Needs practice marks separately.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Small samples.\u003c/b> Two attempts make a rate of 0, 0.5 or 1; wait for at least three.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>No data is not strength.\u003c/b> A module you never practised has no rate; check the untried ones too.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Mixed problems.\u003c/b> All review-set problems belong to this module in the data, so a high rate here means \"recognition\"; use the Pattern line and your error log to see which techniques they were.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Gaming the metric.\u003c/b> Generous Solved marks make the numbers look good and hide exactly the weaknesses you are looking for.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A module has six tried problems: three Solved, two Brute only, one Stuck. What are its help rate and stuck rate, and is it a weak spot?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Help rate (2 + 1) / 6 = 0.5 and stuck rate 1/6, about 0.17. Half the attempts needed help, so it is a candidate; the low stuck rate suggests mostly Brute only results, so work on optimisation (target complexity, the ladder) rather than re-learning the idea.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your mixed review sets show a help rate of 0.6 while every module's own practice is below 0.2. What is the diagnosis?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>You can execute each technique when the module tells you which one to use, but you struggle to choose it unlabelled: a recognition weakness. Spend the next two weeks on classification sprints, the atlas and the look-alike pairs rather than on re-reading technique cards.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your two weakest modules by rate are Advanced dynamic programming (optional) and Sliding window, and an interview is three weeks away. Which comes first, and why?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Sliding window: it is essential, appears in a large share of interview problems and is fast to fix with a few re-solves. Advanced DP is optional and rarely asked; plan it after the interview.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Solve the three problems, then export your progress and run \u003ccode>weak_spots\u003c/code>. Expected: a list of rows \u003ccode>(help rate, module, tried, solved, brute only, stuck)\u003c/code>, worst first, and a written two-week plan for the top row.\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>2966 Divide Array Into Arrays With Max Difference.\u003c/b> Expected: \u003ccode>[[1, 2, 3], [7, 8, 9]]\u003c/code> for \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 7, 8, 9], k = 2\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>[]\u003c/code> for the same array with \u003ccode>k = 1\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>1944 Number of Visible People in a Queue.\u003c/b> Expected: \u003ccode>[2, 1, 2, 1, 0]\u003c/code> for heights \u003ccode>[5, 3, 4, 1, 2]\u003c/code>; O(n).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>2125 Number of Laser Beams in a Bank.\u003c/b> Expected: 6 for \u003ccode>[\"101\", \"000\", \"011\", \"010\"]\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to turn your statuses and error log into a ranked list of weak spots and a two-week practice plan.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","lab:mixed/classification-sprint-twenty-statements-in-thirt":"\u003cp>Recognition is a skill you can drill without writing code. This sprint uses twenty short statements written for this guide: each is a disguised version of a common problem shape, and several come in look-alike pairs on purpose.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Set a timer for 30 minutes. For each statement below write four things on paper, without opening any answer: the \u003cb>technique\u003c/b>, the \u003cb>module\u003c/b>, the \u003cb>deciding cue\u003c/b> (the phrase or constraint that decided it) and the \u003cb>target complexity\u003c/b>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do all twenty before checking anything. If you are stuck on one for more than two minutes, write your best guess and move on: guessing under time pressure is part of the drill.\u003c/li>\u003cli>When the timer ends, open the answers one by one. Score one point for the technique and one for the deciding cue (a different cue that also decides it counts).\u003c/li>\u003cli>For every miss, add a line to your error log with the category \u003ccode>technique\u003c/code> and the cue you missed, and add the cue to your atlas or as a card.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Repeat the sprint in two weeks with the order reversed, and compare scores and times.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>The twenty statements\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">1. A sensor log holds up to 10^5 readings, some of them negative. Count the stretches of consecutive readings that add up to exactly T.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Prefix sums with a hash map of counts (Prefix sums and difference arrays). Deciding cue: an exact target with negative values, so a window cannot decide when to shrink; count earlier running totals equal to total - T. O(n).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">2. Given a string of up to 10^5 lowercase letters, find the length of the longest stretch that uses at most three distinct letters.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Variable sliding window with letter counts (Sliding window). Deciding cue: longest contiguous stretch under a condition that stays true when the stretch shrinks (\"at most three distinct\"). O(n).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">3. A sorted list of prices and a budget B: find two different items whose prices add up to exactly B, using O(1) extra memory.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Two pointers from both ends (Two pointers). Deciding cue: sorted input, a pair with a target, and O(1) extra space, which rules out a hash set. O(n).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">4. Parcels with weights must ship in the given order over d days; each day's load cannot exceed the truck's capacity. Find the smallest capacity that ships everything within d days (n up to 10^5).\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Binary search on the answer with a greedy feasibility check (Binary search and searching the answer). Deciding cue: \"smallest capacity that works\", and a larger capacity never hurts, so feasibility is monotone. O(n log(sum of weights)).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">5. For each day in a list of share prices, report how many days you would wait for a strictly higher price (0 if it never comes).\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Monotonic stack (Monotonic stacks and queues). Deciding cue: \"the next strictly higher value to the right\" for every element. O(n).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">6. Jobs arrive at a server over time, each with a priority. At every tick the server runs the waiting job with the highest priority. Simulate 10^5 arrivals.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Heap (Heaps and priority queues). Deciding cue: repeatedly take the best item while new items keep arriving. O(n log n).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">7. Given 10^5 exam scores that never change, report the 100th highest score once.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Selection: \u003ccode>heapq.nlargest(100, scores)[-1]\u003c/code>, quickselect or a sort (Sorting, partitioning and selection). Deciding cue: one question on fixed data; a heap kept up to date over time is not needed. O(n log k) with nlargest, O(n) on average with quickselect.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">8. Given the start and end times of many meetings, find the smallest number of rooms so that no two overlapping meetings share a room.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Sweep line over +1/-1 events, or a min-heap of end times (Intervals and sweep line). Deciding cue: start and end pairs, and the answer is the peak overlap. O(n log n).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">9. A family tree lists each person's wealth. Find the largest total wealth of any person together with all of their descendants.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Tree recursion returning subtree sums up (Binary trees and tree recursion). Deciding cue: each answer combines a node with the answers of its children. O(n).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">10. Store 10^5 file paths, then answer many queries of the form \"how many stored paths begin with this prefix?\".\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Trie with a count at each node (Tries). Deciding cue: many prefix queries over a large shared vocabulary. O(length) per query. (A sorted list with two bisects also works for static data.)\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">11. In a city grid with walls, find the fewest moves from the entrance to any exit; every move takes one minute.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Breadth-first search, multi-source from all exits or single-source from the entrance (Graph modelling and traversal). Deciding cue: fewest moves when every move costs the same. O(cells).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">12. Roads between depots have different travel times, all positive. Find the quickest time from the main depot to every shop.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Dijkstra with a heap (Weighted shortest paths). Deciding cue: weighted edges with non-negative costs, single source. O((V + E) log V).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">13. Courses have prerequisites. Produce an order in which to take all of them, or report that no order exists.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Topological sort with Kahn's algorithm or DFS (Graph modelling and traversal). Deciding cue: dependency order, and \"no order\" means a cycle. O(V + E).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">14. Friendship links are added one at a time. After each addition, report how many separate friend groups exist.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Union-find with a component counter (Union-find and minimum spanning trees). Deciding cue: links only added, and only group membership is asked; each successful union lowers the count by one. Near O(1) per link.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">15. From at most 12 distinct numbers, list every selection whose sum is exactly T.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Backtracking with pruning (Complete search and backtracking). Deciding cue: \"list every\" and a tiny n; the output itself can be exponential. O(2^n · n) in the worst case.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">16. Given up to 100 positive numbers, each at most 100, count the selections whose sum is exactly T (T up to 10^4), modulo 10^9 + 7.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>0/1 knapsack counting DP (DP on grids, strings and knapsacks). Deciding cue: only a count is asked, with a small target sum, so states (items used, sum) repeat; loop the sums downwards so each number is used once. O(n · T).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">17. Daily prices: make as many buy-then-sell trades as you like, but after selling you must wait one day before buying again. Maximise the profit.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>State-machine DP with states holding, just sold and resting (Dynamic programming: from recursion to tables). Deciding cue: a mode that changes what you may do next (the cooldown). O(n), O(1) space.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">18. Children each need a biscuit of at least their own appetite size; each biscuit goes to at most one child. Maximise the number of satisfied children.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Greedy after sorting both lists, matching the smallest adequate biscuit to each child in order (Greedy algorithms and exchange arguments). Deciding cue: an exchange argument shows giving the smallest biscuit that fits is never worse. O(n log n).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">19. Build a cache with get and put in O(1) that removes the least recently used key when it is full.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Hash map plus a doubly linked list, or OrderedDict with move_to_end (Designing data structures). Deciding cue: \"implement a class\" with O(1) for several operations at once, including an order-based eviction.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">20. In a list where every value appears exactly twice except one, find the lone value using O(1) extra memory.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>XOR of all values (Bits and number theory). Deciding cue: pairs cancel and O(1) memory rules out a set; x ^ x = 0 and x ^ 0 = x. O(n).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A first attempt usually takes 25 to 35 minutes; under 20 means you are ready for the review sets at full speed, over 35 means more atlas work first.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Most misses come from the look-alike pairs placed on purpose: 1 and 2 (prefix sums or window), 6 and 7 (heap or selection), 11 and 12 (BFS or Dijkstra), 15 and 16 (backtracking or DP), 17 and 18 (DP or greedy). If you missed one side of a pair, re-read its row in the look-alike card.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The error log gains one \u003ccode>technique\u003c/code> line per miss, each naming the cue you overlooked. On the repeat sprint two weeks later the score should rise and the time fall; if a pair is still missed, make a card for its deciding question.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>","p:219":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Report whether some value repeats with its two positions at most k apart\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When you meet a value, which earlier occurrence of it is the only one worth comparing with?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Hash map of last positions, or a set over the last k values (Hash tables, sets and counting)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Only the most recent previous index of each value matters, because it is the closest. Keep a dict from value to last index and check i - last[x] &lt;= k before updating it; or keep a set of the last k values and remove the one that falls out of range.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(min(n, k)) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1679":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Remove as many disjoint pairs whose sum is exactly k as possible\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For a value x only one partner value works. How many pairs can x and its partner form together?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Counting complements (Hash tables, sets and counting), or sort and two pointers (Two pointers)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Different value classes never compete: x can only pair with k - x. With a Counter add min(count[x], count[k - x]) for x &lt; k - x, and count[x] // 2 when 2x = k. Sorting and moving two pointers inwards also works: if the sum is too big, the largest value has no partner left.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time with a Counter, O(n log n) with sorting\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:930":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count the subarrays of a 0/1 array whose sum is exactly goal\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A subarray's sum is the difference of two running totals. For the current total, which earlier totals complete a subarray with the right sum?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix sums with a count map (Prefix sums and difference arrays)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep a Counter of running totals seen so far, seeded with {0: 1}; at each index add count[total - goal], then record the total. Because the values are 0 or 1, atMost(goal) - atMost(goal - 1) with a shrinking window also works.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space (O(1) extra with the window version)\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:438":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Find every start position where a stretch of s is a rearrangement of p\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every match has exactly len(p) letters. What changes when the stretch moves one place to the right?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Fixed-size sliding window with letter counts (Sliding window)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Slide a window of length len(p) over s with 26 letter counts: add the entering letter, remove the leaving one, and record the start whenever the counts equal those of p. Tracking how many of the 26 counts currently match makes each step O(1).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) extra space (26 counts)\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:189":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Shift every element k places to the right, wrapping around, in place\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>After the shift the array is two blocks that have swapped places. Which simple in-place operation on a range reverses the order of things?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> In-place reversals (Arrays, dynamic arrays, strings and grids)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Set k %= n, reverse the whole array, then reverse the first k elements and the remaining n - k. The full reversal puts the two blocks in the right places but each one backwards; the two partial reversals fix their insides.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1657":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Decide whether two words can be made equal by swapping letters and by exchanging the roles of two letters everywhere\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>List what the two operations can never change about a word.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Comparing invariants with counts (Hash tables, sets and counting)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Swaps keep the multiset of letters; exchanging two letters swaps their counts. So the words are close exactly when they use the same set of letters and their sorted lists of letter counts are equal (a letter missing from one word can never appear, since exchanges only use existing letters).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) extra space (26 counts)\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1004":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Longest run of 1s if you may flip at most k zeros\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Restate it without flipping: which stretches of the array are acceptable as they are?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Variable sliding window (Sliding window)\u003c/p>\u003cp>A stretch with at most k zeros can be turned into all 1s, so find the longest such stretch. Grow the right end, count zeros, and advance the left end while the count exceeds k; a stretch with too many zeros stays bad as it grows, so the left end never moves back.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:523":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Is there a stretch of at least two numbers whose sum is a multiple of k?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When do two running totals differ by a multiple of k?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix sums modulo k with a first-index map (Prefix sums and difference arrays)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Two running totals with the same remainder mod k enclose a multiple of k. Store the first index of each remainder, seeding remainder 0 at index -1, and report success when a remainder reappears at least two positions later; keeping only the first index makes each candidate stretch as long as possible.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(min(n, k)) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:16":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Choose three numbers whose sum is as close as possible to a target\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Put the numbers in order first. With one number fixed, if the other two add up to too much, which of them should change?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Sort, fix one, two pointers (Two pointers)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Sort, fix i, and move lo and hi inwards: a sum above the target can only get closer by lowering hi, one below it by raising lo. Track the closest sum seen; an exact match can return at once.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n^2) time, O(1) extra space beyond sorting\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:2125":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count connections between devices on different rows when every row between them is empty\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Do the empty rows matter at all? Which pairs of rows actually connect?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Row aggregation on a grid (Arrays, dynamic arrays, strings and grids)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Drop the empty rows; each remaining row connects only with the next non-empty one, and every pair of devices across them is one beam. Sum count[r] * count[next] over consecutive non-empty rows.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(mn) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:946":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Given a push order and a pop order of distinct values, could one stack have produced both?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Replay the operations. After each push, when is a pop forced, and why is it never wrong to pop as early as possible?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Stack simulation (Linked lists, stacks and queues)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Simulate: push each value, then pop while the top equals the next value of the pop order. Popping early is safe because a top value that is due next can never be popped later once something is pushed over it. Valid exactly when the stack ends empty.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:240":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Search a matrix whose rows and columns are each sorted ascending, where rows do not continue one another\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Find a corner where one comparison with the target rules out a whole row or a whole column.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Staircase search on a sorted matrix (Binary search and searching the answer)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Start at the top-right corner. If the value is larger than the target, everything below it in that column is larger too, so move left; if smaller, everything to its left in that row is smaller, so move down. Each step discards a row or a column: at most m + n steps.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(m + n) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1944":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For each person in a line, count the people to their right they can see over everyone in between\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>If a taller person stands closer, who behind them disappears from view for everyone further left?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Monotonic stack (Monotonic stacks and queues)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Scan from the right keeping a stack of heights still visible, decreasing from bottom to top. For person i, pop every shorter height (each is visible to i and hidden by i from everyone further left) and count it; if the stack is still non-empty the next taller person is visible too, so add one. Then push i.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:328":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Reorder a linked list so the nodes in odd positions come first, then the even ones, in place\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Walk once and grow two separate chains. What must you remember in order to join them at the end?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Pointer rewiring on a linked list (Linked lists, stacks and queues)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep the odd tail, the even tail and the even head; repeatedly set odd.next = even.next and even.next = odd.next, advancing both, until even or even.next is None. Then attach the even head after the odd tail.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:2300":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For each spell, count the potions whose product with it reaches a success threshold\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For one spell, which potions work? If the potions were in order, what shape would that group have?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Sort once, then binary search per query (Binary search and searching the answer)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Sort the potions once. For spell s the working potions are exactly those &gt;= ceil(success / s), a suffix of the sorted list, so the count is m - bisect_left(potions, (success + s - 1) // s).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O((n + m) log m) time, O(m) for the sorted copy\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1019":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For every node of a linked list, the value of the first later node that is strictly larger\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Copy the values into a list. Which earlier nodes are still waiting for their answer, and how are their values ordered?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Monotonic stack for the next greater value (Monotonic stacks and queues)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Walk the list keeping a stack of indices whose answer is unknown; their values decrease from bottom to top. A new value pops and answers every smaller value on top, then waits itself. Indices left on the stack keep the answer 0.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1248":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count the subarrays that contain exactly k odd numbers\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Only parity matters. Replace each number by 1 if it is odd and 0 if even: what are you counting now?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix sums with a count map after mapping to parity (Prefix sums and difference arrays)\u003c/p>\u003cp>It becomes \"count subarrays with sum exactly k\" on a 0/1 array: a Counter of running totals seeded with {0: 1}, adding count[total - k] at each step; or atMost(k) - atMost(k - 1) with a window.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space (O(1) with the window version)\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1283":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Smallest divisor such that the sum of the rounded-up quotients stays within a threshold\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>As the divisor grows, what happens to the sum? What does that let you do with the whole range of divisors?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Binary search on the answer (Binary search and searching the answer)\u003c/p>\u003cp>The sum never increases as the divisor grows, so \"sum &lt;= threshold\" is False...False True...True over d in [1, max(nums)]. Find the first True with the check sum((x + d - 1) // d) &lt;= threshold, which costs O(n) per probe.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n log max(nums)) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:2966":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Split the numbers into groups of three where every group spans at most k\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Which numbers should share a group? Would putting far-apart values together ever help?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Sort, then group neighbours (Sorting, partitioning and selection)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Sort and cut into consecutive triples. If some consecutive triple spans more than k, no grouping works: any valid grouping can be rearranged into consecutive triples without increasing any group's span (an exchange argument from the smallest element upwards).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n log n) time, O(n) for the output\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1209":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Repeatedly delete k equal adjacent letters until no such block remains\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A deletion can make the letters on either side meet. What do you need to remember about what you have kept so far?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Stack of runs (Linked lists, stacks and queues)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep a stack of [letter, run length] for the output so far. A letter equal to the top's letter increments its count, and the pair is popped when the count reaches k, which exposes the previous run exactly as a cascade needs; otherwise push [letter, 1]. Join the runs at the end.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:572":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Does one tree appear inside another as some node together with all its descendants?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Split it into two questions: where could the copy start, and how do you check that two trees are identical?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Tree recursion with an equality check (Binary trees and tree recursion)\u003c/p>\u003cp>For every node of the large tree, run an \"identical trees\" check against the small one (both recursive). That is O(mn) in the worst case; serialising both trees with markers for missing children and searching for one string inside the other (KMP) makes it O(m + n).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(m·n) time simply, O(m + n) with serialisation; O(height) recursion space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:692":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The k most frequent words, highest count first, ties in alphabetical order\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Can you express the whole ranking rule as one sort key?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Counting plus a size-k heap with a composite key (Heaps and priority queues)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Count with a Counter, then rank by the key (-count, word). sorted(...)[:k] costs O(u log u) for u distinct words; heapq.nsmallest(k, counts, key=lambda w: (-counts[w], w)) costs O(u log k), the follow-up's target.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n + u log k) time, O(u) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1288":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count the intervals that are not contained in another interval\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>In which order should you visit the intervals so that any interval that covers another is seen before it?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Sort, then sweep (Intervals and sweep line)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Sort by start ascending and end descending; then an interval is covered exactly when its end is at most the largest end seen so far. Count the intervals that raise that maximum.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n log n) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:103":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>List the tree's values level by level, alternating left to right and right to left\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Visit the tree one level at a time. What changes between consecutive levels, and what does not?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Breadth-first traversal by levels (Binary trees and tree recursion)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Plain level-order traversal with a queue, one level per loop iteration; reverse every second level's list (or fill it with appendleft on a deque). The direction never changes which nodes form a level, only the order you report them in.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(width) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1029":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Send exactly half of 2n people to each of two cities at minimum total cost\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Suppose everyone starts in city B. What does moving one person to city A cost or save?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Greedy by cost difference after sorting (Greedy algorithms and exchange arguments)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Starting with everyone in B, moving person i to A changes the total by a_i - b_i. Choose the n people with the smallest a_i - b_i: sort by that difference and send the first half to A. Any other choice trades a smaller difference for a larger one.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n log n) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1233":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Remove every folder path that lies inside another listed folder\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When is \"/a/b\" inside \"/a\", and why is \"/ab\" not? In sorted order, where does a sub-folder sit relative to its parent?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix structure: sorted prefix checks or a trie of path parts (Tries)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Every sub-folder of p starts with p + \"/\", and since \"/\" sorts before every letter, all of them come right after p in sorted order. So sort, and keep a path unless it starts with the last kept path + \"/\". A trie of path components that stops at nodes ending a folder works too.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(nL log n) time to sort n paths of length L, O(nL) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1382":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Rebuild a binary search tree so that it is height-balanced, with the same values\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Which traversal lists the values in sorted order? Which value of a sorted list should become the root?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> In-order to a sorted list, then build from the middle (Binary search trees and ordered maps)\u003c/p>\u003cp>In-order traversal yields the sorted values; build recursively with the middle element as the root and the two halves as its subtrees. At every node the halves differ in size by at most one, so the tree is balanced.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1353":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Attend as many events as possible, at most one per day, each on some day inside its range\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Go day by day. Among the events you could attend today, which one is the most urgent?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Sweep the days with a min-heap of end days (Heaps and priority queues)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Sort by start; sweep the days, pushing the end day of every event that starts today onto a min-heap, discarding ends already past, and attending the event with the earliest end. Taking the soonest-ending event is safe: any schedule that uses a later-ending one today can swap them.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O((n + D) log n) time for D days, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:695":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Size of the largest group of land cells connected up, down, left or right\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Start from an unvisited land cell and walk to everything connected to it. Can the walk report how many cells it covered?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Flood fill on a grid graph (Graph modelling and traversal)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Flood fill (iterative DFS or BFS) from each unvisited land cell, marking cells visited and counting them; the answer is the largest count. Every cell is visited once.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(mn) time and space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:2316":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count the pairs of nodes that have no path between them\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Two nodes are unreachable from each other exactly when they lie in different groups. If you knew the group sizes, how would you count the pairs?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Connected components with union-find or DFS (Union-find and minimum spanning trees)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Find the connected components (union-find or DFS) and their sizes; pairs across components are the unreachable ones. Add size * (nodes counted so far) per component, or compute (n^2 - sum of size^2) / 2.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n + E α(n)) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1466":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Re-orient the fewest one-way roads of a tree so that every city can reach city 0\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Walk outwards from city 0 ignoring direction. Which roads point the wrong way?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Traversal with edge directions as weights (Graph modelling and traversal)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Store each road both ways, marking the original direction with cost 1 and the reverse with cost 0; traverse from 0 and add the cost of every edge you use. An original road pointing away from 0 must be flipped, one pointing towards 0 is fine, and in a tree each road is used exactly once.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time and space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:851":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For each person, the quietest person among those known to be at least as rich\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The richer-than relation never loops. If you knew the answer for everyone directly richer than x, could you build x's answer?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> DP over a DAG in topological order (Graph modelling and traversal)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Direct edges from richer to poorer: the graph is acyclic. answer[x] is the quietest of x and answer[y] over everyone y directly richer than x; compute it with a memoised DFS over the richer lists, or push answers along a topological order from the richest.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n + E) time and space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:542":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Distance from every cell to the nearest 0 cell\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>One search per cell is far too slow. Could all the zeros start searching at the same moment?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Multi-source BFS (Graph modelling and traversal)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Multi-source BFS: put every 0 in the queue at distance 0 and expand outwards; the first time a cell is reached gives its distance, because BFS reaches cells in order of distance from the whole set of sources.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(mn) time and space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1786":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count paths from 1 to n along which the shortest distance to n strictly decreases at every step\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Work out the distances to n first. Once you have them, what kind of graph do the allowed moves form?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Dijkstra, then DP in distance order (Weighted shortest paths)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Dijkstra from n gives dist; an allowed move goes from u to a neighbour v with dist[v] &lt; dist[u], so moves can never cycle. Count paths with DP in increasing order of dist: ways[n] = 1 and ways[u] is the sum of ways[v] over allowed neighbours, modulo 10^9 + 7.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(E log V) time, O(V + E) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:909":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Fewest dice rolls to finish a snakes and ladders board\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every roll costs the same. What are the states, and what are the up to six moves from each?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> BFS on an implicit graph (Graph modelling and traversal)\u003c/p>\u003cp>BFS over squares 1..n^2: from square s try s + 1 to s + 6, follow a snake or ladder if one starts there (once only), and record the roll count when a square is first reached. The fiddly part is converting a square number to (row, col), with rows numbered from the bottom and alternating direction.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n^2) time and space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:2492":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Smallest possible weight of the lightest road on any route from 1 to n, where roads may be reused\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Since a route may go back and forth, which roads can a route from 1 to n touch?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Connected component plus a minimum over its edges (Union-find and minimum spanning trees)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Because revisits are allowed, a route can detour through any road of the component that contains city 1 (and n lies in it). The answer is the smallest weight among those roads: find the component with union-find or BFS from 1 and take the minimum weight over its edges.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n + E α(n)) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1882":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Assign queued tasks to free servers by weight and then index, waiting whenever none is free\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>You keep needing two different \"best\" servers: the best free one, and the one that becomes free soonest.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Two heaps for an event simulation (Heaps and priority queues)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep a min-heap of free servers keyed (weight, index) and a min-heap of busy servers keyed (free time, weight, index). For task j at time t = max(t, j), release every busy server free by t; if none is free, jump t to the earliest free time and release those. Pop the best free server and push it as busy until t + tasks[j].\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O((n + m) log n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:2092":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A secret spreads through meetings, and people meeting at the same instant pass it on instantly\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Group the meetings by time. Within one time, who ends up knowing? What should happen to people who met but did not learn it?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Union-find with a reset per time slot (Union-find and minimum spanning trees)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Process the times in increasing order. For one time, union the people who meet; afterwards everyone connected to person 0's set knows the secret, and everyone else from those meetings is reset to a singleton so later meetings do not inherit a false link. A BFS inside each time slot works the same way.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(M log M + M α(n)) time, O(n + M) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:279":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Fewest perfect squares that add up to n\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Suppose you knew the last square used. What problem is left, and how many different leftovers can there be?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Unbounded coin-change DP (Dynamic programming: from recursion to tables)\u003c/p>\u003cp>best[0] = 0 and best[i] = 1 + min(best[i - s*s]) over squares s*s &lt;= i: coin change with square coins, n amounts each trying about sqrt(n) coins. (Number theory says the answer is never more than four, which allows faster special-case solutions.)\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n sqrt n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:40":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>List every combination that sums to the target, each element used at most once, from input with duplicates, with no repeated combinations\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Two equal numbers can produce the same combination twice. At one level of the choices, when is picking a value pointless?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Backtracking with sort-and-skip deduplication (Complete search and backtracking)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Sort, then recurse choosing the next element from index start onwards; skip a value equal to the previous one at the same depth (i &gt; start and a[i] == a[i - 1]), and stop the loop once a[i] exceeds the remaining target, since the rest are larger. Recurse with i + 1 because each element is used once.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> Exponential in the worst case (output size times n); the small target keeps it fast\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1035":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Draw the most non-crossing lines joining equal numbers in two rows\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Two lines do not cross exactly when their endpoints come in the same order in both rows. What are you choosing from each row, then?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Longest common subsequence in disguise (DP on grids, strings and knapsacks)\u003c/p>\u003cp>A set of non-crossing lines is a common subsequence of the two arrays and vice versa, so the answer is their longest common subsequence: dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + 1 on a match, else max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(mn) time, O(min(m, n)) space with two rows\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1594":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Largest non-negative product along a right-and-down path in a grid that contains negative numbers\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A very negative product can become the best one after one more negative cell. What do you need to keep for every cell?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Grid DP with max and min states (DP on grids, strings and knapsacks)\u003c/p>\u003cp>For every cell keep both the largest and the smallest product of a path reaching it; from the top and left neighbours, multiply both by the cell's value and take the max and the min. The answer is the corner's max modulo 10^9 + 7, or -1 if it is negative; take the modulus only at the end.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(mn) time, O(n) space with one row\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:93":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every way to split a digit string into a valid IPv4 address\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Exactly four parts, each 1 to 3 digits with value 0 to 255 and no leading zero. How many ways can there possibly be?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Backtracking with pruning (Complete search and backtracking)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Try each part length 1, 2 or 3 recursively, rejecting parts above 255 or with a leading zero, and prune when the remaining digits cannot fill the remaining parts (fewer than one or more than three per part). There are at most 3^4 = 81 length choices.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(1): at most 81 candidate splits, each checked in O(length)\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:2140":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Answer questions in order; solving one earns points but skips the next brainpower questions\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A decision only affects what comes after it. From which end is it easier to start?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Take-or-skip DP from the back (Dynamic programming: from recursion to tables)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Solve from the back: dp[i] = max(dp[i + 1], points[i] + dp[i + brainpower[i] + 1]), with dp = 0 past the end. Each question is a take-or-skip choice whose consequences all lie later.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:375":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Least money that guarantees a win at higher-or-lower guessing when a wrong guess x costs x\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>You choose the guess; the side is chosen against you. What does the guaranteed cost of a range depend on?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Interval DP with a minimax step (Advanced dynamic programming; memoised recursion over ranges is enough)\u003c/p>\u003cp>cost(l, r) = min over guesses g in [l, r] of g + max(cost(l, g - 1), cost(g + 1, r)), with cost 0 on ranges of size 0 or 1. Fill by increasing range length or memoise: a minimax over sub-ranges.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n^3) time, O(n^2) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:646":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Longest chain of pairs where each pair starts after the previous one ends\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Which pair should start the chain? Think about which first choice leaves the most room for the rest.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Greedy by earliest end (Greedy algorithms and exchange arguments)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Sort by end and take each pair whose start is greater than the end of the last pair taken (activity selection). The pair that ends first can replace the first pair of any optimal chain, so taking it is safe. An O(n^2) DP also passes but is not needed.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n log n) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:712":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Smallest total of character codes deleted to make two strings equal\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The characters you keep must appear in the same order in both strings. What is the most valuable thing you can keep?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Weighted longest common subsequence (DP on grids, strings and knapsacks)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Minimising the deleted value is maximising the kept value: dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + ord(c) on a match, else the larger of dropping one character from either side. The answer is the codes of both strings minus twice the best kept value (or fill deletion costs directly with min).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(mn) time, O(n) space with two rows\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1653":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Fewest deletions so that no 'b' appears before an 'a'\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When you reach an 'a', there are two ways to make the prefix acceptable. What does each cost?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> One-pass DP over prefixes (Dynamic programming: from recursion to tables)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Scan with b_count, the number of b's so far, and best, the answer for the prefix. On 'b', b_count += 1; on 'a', either delete this 'a' (best + 1) or delete every 'b' so far (b_count): best = min(best + 1, b_count). Equivalently, try every split point: b's before it plus a's after it.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1140":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Players take all the stones in the first X piles, 1 &lt;= X &lt;= 2M, after which M becomes max(M, X); maximise the first player's total\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Whatever has happened, the rest of the game depends only on where the remaining piles start and on M. The total of the remaining piles is fixed.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Game DP over (position, M) (Advanced dynamic programming; memoisation is enough)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Let f(i, M) be the most the player to move can collect from piles i onwards. Then f(i, M) = suffix[i] - min over x in 1..2M of f(i + x, max(M, x)), and f(i, M) = suffix[i] when i + 2M &gt;= n. Memoise over (i, M).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n^3) time, O(n^2) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:688":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Probability that a knight is still on an n x n board after k uniformly random moves\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Instead of following single paths, spread probability over the board one move at a time.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Probability DP over steps (DP on grids, strings and knapsacks)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep a board of probabilities. For each move, every cell sends one eighth of its probability to each of its eight knight targets that are still on the board (mass that leaves is dropped). After k moves, sum the board.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(k n^2) time (8 moves per cell), O(n^2) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1442":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count triples i &lt; j &lt;= k where the XOR of a[i..j-1] equals the XOR of a[j..k]\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>If two values are equal, what is their XOR? What does that say about the whole stretch from i to k?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix XOR (Bits and number theory, with prefix sums)\u003c/p>\u003cp>a == b exactly when a ^ b = 0, that is when the XOR of a[i..k] is 0, which with running XORs means prefix[i] == prefix[k + 1]. Then every j in (i, k] works: k - i choices. Sum over equal-prefix pairs in O(n^2), or in O(n) with dicts holding the count and the index sum per prefix value.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time with dicts (O(n^2) is plenty for n &lt;= 300), O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1823":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Friends sit in a circle and every k-th one leaves; who remains?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>After the first friend leaves, the game restarts with n - 1 friends from a new position. How are the two answers related?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Josephus recurrence (Bits and number theory; recursion)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Number from 0. The survivor among n is the survivor among n - 1 shifted by k: f(1) = 0, f(n) = (f(n - 1) + k) % n, and the answer is f(n) + 1. Simulating with a list and pop((i + k - 1) % len) is O(n^2), also fine for n &lt;= 500.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1352":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A stream of numbers with queries for the product of the last k\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Running products let you divide out the part you do not want. What does a zero do to that idea?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix products with a reset at zero (Designing data structures, with prefix sums)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep a list P of running products since the last zero, starting [1]. On add(0) reset P to [1]; otherwise append P[-1] * x. A query k returns 0 if k &gt;= len(P) (a zero lies among the last k) and P[-1] // P[-1 - k] otherwise.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(1) per operation, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:2101":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Set off one bomb; every exploding bomb sets off all bombs within its own radius; maximise the total\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Does \"a reaches b\" imply \"b reaches a\"? What structure does that suggest?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Directed reachability by a search from each source (Graph modelling and traversal)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Build a directed graph with an edge i -&gt; j when (xi - xj)^2 + (yi - yj)^2 &lt;= ri^2 (integers, no square roots), then run BFS or DFS from every bomb and take the largest reachable set. Union-find is wrong here, because reach is not symmetric.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n^3) time (n searches over up to n^2 edges), O(n^2) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:382":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Return a uniformly random value from a linked list, possibly of unknown length\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Can you decide on the fly, as the nodes go past, whether the current node should replace your pick?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Reservoir sampling over a stream (Bits and number theory: randomness and counting)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Simplest: copy the values into a list once and use random.choice (O(n) space, O(1) per call). Without extra space, reservoir sampling: keep the i-th node with probability 1/i; node i survives every later step with probability (1/i)·(i/(i+1))···((n-1)/n) = 1/n.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) per call and O(1) space with the reservoir, or O(1) per call and O(n) space with a list\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1856":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Maximise min(subarray) × sum(subarray) over all subarrays\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Fix the element that acts as the minimum. How far can its subarray stretch?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Monotonic stack bounds plus prefix sums (Monotonic stacks and queues)\u003c/p>\u003cp>For each i, the best subarray with a[i] as its minimum spans from just after the previous smaller element to just before the next smaller one (values are positive, so wider is better). Find both bounds with a monotonic stack and get range sums from prefix sums; maximise before taking the modulus.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:2349":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Change the number stored at an index; query the smallest index currently holding a given number\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Changes leave old entries behind. Do you have to clean them up straight away?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Heap with lazy deletion (Designing data structures)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep index -&gt; number in a dict and number -&gt; min-heap of indices. On change, update the dict and push the index onto the new number's heap. On find, pop from the top while the top index no longer holds that number (lazy deletion), then return the top or -1.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(log n) amortised per operation, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:2402":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Meetings take the lowest-numbered free room or wait for the room that frees first, keeping their length; which room hosts the most?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>At each meeting you need either the lowest-numbered free room or the room that becomes free soonest: two different orders.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Two heaps for room allocation (Heaps and priority queues)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Sort the meetings by start. Keep a min-heap of free room numbers and a min-heap of (end time, room) for busy rooms. Before each meeting release the rooms with end &lt;= start; if a room is free take the lowest, else pop the earliest (end, room) and run the meeting from that end for its original length. Count meetings per room.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(m log m + m log n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1964":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For each position, the longest non-decreasing course that ends exactly there\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Keep, for every course length seen so far, the lowest height a course of that length can end with. How does a new obstacle change that list?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Patience-style longest non-decreasing subsequence (Dynamic programming: from recursion to tables)\u003c/p>\u003cp>The list of lowest ending heights is non-decreasing, so binary search it: the new obstacle extends the longest course whose ending height is &lt;= it (bisect_right, because equal heights may follow), replaces the entry at that position, and its answer is that position + 1.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n log n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1146":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>An array with snapshots; read any index as it was at any snapshot\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Copying the array at every snapshot is too expensive. What is the least you could store per index?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Per-key version history with binary search (Designing data structures)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Per index keep a list of (snap_id, value), appended on set (overwrite the last entry if it has the same snap_id). get(i, s) binary-searches that list for the last entry with snap_id &lt;= s; an index never set before s returns 0.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(1) set, O(log S) get, O(n + number of sets) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>"}]);
